{"items":[{"owner":{"display_name":"Leo Ely","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/32444963/leo-ely"},"score":0,"last_activity_date":1785990931,"creation_date":1785990931,"answer_id":79990880,"question_id":33957477,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/33957477/cross-domain-localstorage-with-javascript/79990880#79990880","body":"<p>The URL's search params on this website are accessible and modifiable.When reading and writing the URL's search params, as long as a unique key is used, it will not affect the website's functionality.Moreover,URL's search params supports cross-domain read/write.Therefore, it can serve as a small amount of cross-domain storage.The advantage is that there are no other expenses.And it's very simple.</p>\n<p>1.<strong>URL's search params</strong> reading method.</p>\n<pre><code>new URL(window.location).searchParams.get('key');\n</code></pre>\n<p>2.<strong>URL's search params</strong> writing method.</p>\n<pre><code>let url = new URL(&quot;https://example.com?foo=1&amp;bar=2&quot;);\nlet params = new URLSearchParams(url.search);\n\n// Add a third parameter.\nparams.set(&quot;baz&quot;, 3);\nparams.toString(); // &quot;foo=1&amp;bar=2&amp;baz=3&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>3.Can refer this answer <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/10970078/modifying-a-query-string-without-reloading-the-page\">Modifying a query string without reloading the page</a>.Setting the <strong>URL's search params</strong> will not refresh the page.</p>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"David Brierton","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/4106374/david-brierton"},"score":0,"last_activity_date":1784740509,"creation_date":1784740509,"answer_id":79983928,"question_id":79983514,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79983514/how-to-invalidate-cloudfront-path-if-the-filename-contains-an-apostrophe/79983928#79983928","body":"<p>This issue is <code>URLSearchParams</code>. That creates a query string and encodes the leading <code>/</code> so cloudfront no longer sees a valid invalidation path.</p>\n<p>Encode each part of the path instead and then join it back together</p>\n<pre><code>import {\n   CloudFrontClient,\n   CreateInvalidationCommand,\n} from '@aws-sdk/client-cloudfront';\n\nexport async function invalidateCacheForFile(filePath: string) {\n   const cloudfrontClient = new CloudFrontClient(config);\n\n   const path = filePath.startsWith('/') ? filePath : `/${filePath}`;\n\n   const encodedPath = path\n       .split('/')\n       .map(part =&gt; encodeURIComponent(part).replace(/'/g, '%27'))\n       .join('/');\n\n   await cloudfrontClient.send(\n       new CreateInvalidationCommand({\n           DistributionId: 'some-distribution',\n           InvalidationBatch: {\n               Paths: {\n                   Quantity: 1,\n                   Items: [encodedPath],\n               },\n               CallerReference: Date.now().toString(),\n           },\n       })\n   );\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>That keeps the <code>/</code> characters in place while encoding the apostrophe and any other special characters in the filename.</p>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"Naren Murali","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/5924562/naren-murali"},"score":6,"last_activity_date":1784715746,"creation_date":1784715746,"answer_id":79983817,"question_id":79983810,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79983810/httpclient-updates-component-state-but-template-doesnt-refresh-after-api-respo/79983817#79983817","body":"<p>Since you are using Angular 21 which is by default zoneless, we need to use <code>signals</code> when you want to use the variable in the DOM or View (So that the updates are reflected in the view) another reason to use signals is when the state change needs to have reactivity of updating other states, like using <code>computed</code>, <code>effect</code>, etc.</p>\n<p>You should also simplify the API calls, by using the resource API, for example we can simplify the code using <code>httpResource</code>.</p>\n<h4>TS:</h4>\n<pre><code>export class GetApi implements OnInit {\n  private readonly http = inject(HttpClient);\n  public todoResource = httpResource(() =&gt; `https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos`);\n}\n</code></pre>\n<h4>HTML:</h4>\n<pre><code>@if(todoResource.error(); as error) {\n   &lt;p&gt;Unknown API error&lt;/p&gt;\n} @else if(todoResource.isLoading()) {\n  &lt;p&gt; Loading... &lt;/p&gt;\n} @else {\n@let todoList = todoResource.data();\n&lt;p&gt;Total: {{ todoList.length }}&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;pre&gt;{{ todoList | json }}&lt;/pre&gt;\n\n&lt;table border=&quot;1&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;thead&gt;\n        &lt;tr&gt;\n            &lt;th&gt;User&lt;/th&gt;\n            &lt;th&gt;Id&lt;/th&gt;\n            &lt;th&gt;Title&lt;/th&gt;\n            &lt;th&gt;Completed&lt;/th&gt;\n        &lt;/tr&gt;\n    &lt;/thead&gt;\n    &lt;tbody&gt;\n        @for (item of todoList; track item.id) {\n            &lt;tr&gt;\n                &lt;td&gt;{{ item.userId }}&lt;/td&gt;\n                &lt;td&gt;{{ item.id }}&lt;/td&gt;\n                &lt;td&gt;{{ item.title }}&lt;/td&gt;\n                &lt;td&gt;{{ item.completed }}&lt;/td&gt;\n            &lt;/tr&gt;\n        }\n    &lt;/tbody&gt;\n&lt;/table&gt;\n}\n</code></pre>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"Manely Sabouri","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/22205748/manely-sabouri"},"score":1,"last_activity_date":1782725749,"creation_date":1782725749,"answer_id":79970228,"question_id":51095254,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/51095254/success-message-after-form-submission/79970228#79970228","body":"<p>You can't show an alert after <code>return true</code> in the same request. Once the form is submitted, the browser leaves the current page and the JavaScript context is destroyed.</p>\n<p>If you need to display a success message after the page reloads, pass a flag (for example, via a query string) and check for it when the page loads:</p>\n<pre><code>window.onload = function () {\n    const params = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);\n\n    if (params.get('success') === '1') {\n        alert('Form submitted successfully.');\n    }\n};\n</code></pre>\n<p>Then redirect to something like:</p>\n<pre><code>page.php?success=1\n</code></pre>\n<p>Also note that showing a success message in <code>onsubmit</code> only means the client-side validation passed. It does not mean the server successfully processed the form.</p>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"Ray Wallace","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/20901722/ray-wallace"},"score":1,"last_activity_date":1780123004,"last_edit_date":1780123004,"creation_date":1716830929,"answer_id":78540407,"question_id":78539357,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/78539357/error-on-converting-json-data-to-qr-code-using-js-library/78540407#78540407","body":"<p>I don't know how you got your code to give you even one value in the QR code. Your code as is, was not runnable at all for me.</p>\n<p>There were two major problems:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Your const and var definitions need to be moved out of the .qrcode() function body.</li>\n<li>Setting your myJSON was all wrong. You did not end up with any of your data in it.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Here is the edited code that worked for me:</p>\n<pre><code>// NOTE Your const a var definitions need to be moved out of the .qrcode() function body\n\nconst queryString = window.location.search;\n\nconst urlParams = new URLSearchParams(queryString);\n\n// NOTE You do not need the .toString(), the parameters are already strings\nconst id = urlParams.get('id').toString();\n\nconst  tableid = urlParams.get('tableid').toString();\n\nconst  areacode = urlParams.get('areacode').toString();\n\nconst  catid = urlParams.get('catid').toString();\n\n\n// NOTE This line has a misplace ' at the end\n// NOTE Even then it does not give you anything useful\n//   var myJSON={&quot;id&quot;:&quot;'+id+'&quot;,&quot;tableid&quot;:&quot;'+tableid+'&quot;,&quot;areacode&quot;:&quot;'+areacode+'&quot;,&quot;catid&quot;:&quot;'+catid+&quot;}';\nvar myJSON = JSON.stringify( {id, tableid, areacode, catid} )\n\njQuery('#output').qrcode( {\n  // version range somewhere in 1 .. 40\n  minVersion: 1,\n  maxVersion: 40,\n\n  // error correction level: 'L', 'M', 'Q' or 'H'\n  ecLevel: 'Q',\n\n  // offset in pixel if drawn onto existing canvas\n  left: 0,\n  top: 0,\n\n  // size in pixel\n  size: 500,\n\n  // code color or image element\n  fill: '#000',\n\n  // corner radius relative to module width: 0.0 .. 0.5\n  radius: 0,\n\n  // quiet zone in modules\n  quiet: 0,\n  text: myJSON\n\n} );\n\n// NOTE This is an extraneous line\n// });\n</code></pre>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"ggorlen","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/6243352/ggorlen"},"score":0,"last_activity_date":1779430110,"last_edit_date":1779430110,"creation_date":1708497620,"answer_id":78032001,"question_id":36478149,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/36478149/p5js-modifying-local-file-such-as-json-or-xml-or-table/78032001#78032001","body":"<p>Numerous approaches for data persistence exist. Deciding which approach to take depends on your needs. Begin by clarifying what data you need to persist and the scope of data access (single user or all users). Another consideration is complexity, particularly for learners who are new to coding.</p>\n<p>For simple use cases, like persisting a user's preferences, or building a per-user high scores table, I'd start with <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/localStorage\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>localStorage</code></a>. It's simple to use and well-supported. The disadvantage is that it's specific to a single user (although that may be all you need). If the user clears their local browser files, local storage will be cleared.</p>\n<p>The API is simple:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-js prettyprint-override\"><code>// set a key to a value\nlocalStorage.setItem(&quot;score&quot;, score);\n\n// get the value for a key\nconst score = localStorage.getItem(&quot;score&quot;);\n</code></pre>\n<p>For more complex data, like an array or object, you can use <code>JSON.stringify()</code> and serialize the data structure going into local storage and <code>JSON.parse()</code> to restore it after pulling it out.</p>\n<p>If you want to use a backend server so you can share data across clients, SQLite is a good starter database, but this is still pretty complex for most beginners since it involves SQL. Avoiding SQL can be done using an object relational mapper (ORM) like Sequelize, but such libraries also tend to have a steep learning curve.</p>\n<p>A JSON file is not scalable for serious apps, but should be far more accessible to learners and is a great entry point to backend development. I'll add an example below you can run and modify to meet your needs.</p>\n<p>Here's the server.js code, which uses a single library called Express, which makes it easier to develop a server application:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-js prettyprint-override\"><code>const express = require(&quot;express&quot;);\nconst fs = require(&quot;fs&quot;);\n\nconst scoresPath = &quot;scores.json&quot;;\nconst scores = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(scoresPath, {encoding: &quot;utf-8&quot;}));\nconst app = express();\napp\n  .set(&quot;port&quot;, process.env.PORT || 5000)\n\n  // serve the sketch HTML and other static assets\n  // from the public/ directory, if you want\n  .use(express.static(&quot;public&quot;))\n\n  // ...or enable requests from other origins if\n  // your sketch isn't on the same server\n  .use((request, response, next) =&gt; {\n    response.set(&quot;Access-Control-Allow-Origin&quot;, &quot;*&quot;);\n    next();\n  })\n\n  // a single endpoint that can accept data in the form\n  // /scores?name=foo&amp;score=42\n  // or returns all scores if no query parameters are provided\n  .get(&quot;/scores&quot;, (request, response) =&gt; {\n    if (request.query.name &amp;&amp; request.query.score) {\n      scores[request.query.name] = +request.query.score;\n      fs.writeFile(scoresPath, JSON.stringify(scores), (error, result) =&gt; {\n        if (error) {\n          console.error(error);\n        }\n      });\n    }\n\n    response.json(scores);\n  })\n  .listen(app.get(&quot;port&quot;), () =&gt;\n    console.log(&quot;listening on port&quot;, app.get(&quot;port&quot;))\n  );\n</code></pre>\n<p>and create a scores.json data file with an empty object in it:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-json prettyprint-override\"><code>{}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's the p5.js script (public/index.html):</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-html prettyprint-override\"><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=&quot;en&quot;&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n&lt;title&gt;P5.js client-server persistence example&lt;/title&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n&lt;script src=&quot;https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/p5.js/1.9.0/p5.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;script&gt;\n\nlet scores;\nlet scoresEl;\n\nfunction randomString(n=10) {\n  let res = &quot;&quot;;\n\n  for (let i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) {\n    res += String.fromCharCode(floor(random(97, 122)));\n  }\n\n  return res;\n}\n\nfunction renderScores() {\n  scoresEl.elt.textContent = JSON.stringify(scores, null, 2);\n}\n\nfunction addScore() {\n  const params = new URLSearchParams({\n    score: floor(random(0, 100)),\n    name: randomString(),\n  });\n  scores = loadJSON(`scores?${params.toString()}`, renderScores);\n}\n\nfunction setup() {\n  scoresEl = createElement(&quot;pre&quot;, &quot;Loading scores...&quot;);\n  scoresEl.position(5, 30);\n  scores = loadJSON(&quot;scores&quot;, renderScores);\n  const newScoreBtn = createButton(&quot;Add new score&quot;); \n  newScoreBtn.position(5, 5);\n  newScoreBtn.mousePressed(addScore); \n}\n\n&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Install Node on your system and open a terminal, then run</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-bash prettyprint-override\"><code>npm init -y\nnpm i express@4.18.2\nnode server\n</code></pre>\n<p>With the server running, navigate to http://localhost:5000 to run the client app and click the button to add a few scores. Scores should persist even after cycling the server on and off, and you'll see the scores.json file updating.</p>\n<p>I've tried to keep the code as simple as possible, but there's still a lot going on here. Try making adjustments and researching the individual concepts in isolation, like JSON file reading and writing, creating routes in Express, and so on. Adjust the app to work with a simple game of your own creation.</p>\n<p>In the interest of keeping things compact, I've avoided most error handling. For complex data, a POST route is preferable to a GET route as I used here, but it's a little bit more complex to implement.</p>\n<p>You can run the server locally, or you can code and host the server on a free tier on a hosting site like Render, although there may be restrictions, like slow startup time. Always-on server apps usually cost money.</p>\n<p>You don't have to use Node.js for the backend, although it's probably the easiest option for those already using JS on the frontend. Hosting on a site like Python Anywhere and developing your code using Python and Flask may be appealing to those who have experience with it.</p>\n<p>Firebase or Supabase are other persistence options to consider.</p>\n<p>Keep in mind, p5 is just a rendering library for HTML5, so anything that works for browser JS will work for p5 as well.</p>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"Sharik Wani","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/32735888/sharik-wani"},"score":1,"last_activity_date":1778944350,"creation_date":1778944350,"answer_id":79942197,"question_id":79942195,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79942195/fetch-api-returning-undefined-data-inside-async-function/79942197#79942197","body":"<p>Your <code>data</code> is not the actual JSON yet because <code>response.json()</code> also returns a <strong>Promise</strong>. You need to <code>await</code> it.</p>\n<p>Use this:</p>\n<pre><code>async function getUsers() {\n    try {\n        const response = await fetch(&quot;https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users&quot;);\n\n        if (!response.ok) {\n            throw new Error(`HTTP error! Status: ${response.status}`);\n        }\n\n        const data = await response.json();\n\n        console.log(data);\n        return data;\n    } catch (error) {\n        console.error(&quot;Failed to fetch users:&quot;, error);\n    }\n}\n\ngetUsers();\n</code></pre>\n<p>Also renember if you want to use the returned data outside the function, you must either <code>await getUsers()</code> inside another async function or use <code>.then()</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>getUsers().then(users =&gt; {\n    console.log(users);\n});\n</code></pre>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"Abdulla Nilam","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/4595675/abdulla-nilam"},"score":0,"last_activity_date":1778148157,"creation_date":1778148157,"answer_id":79937497,"question_id":79937456,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79937456/how-can-fix-this-problem-from-json-placeholder-ullrich/79937497#79937497","body":"<p>If you use</p>\n<pre><code>const todos = await todoResponse.json()\nconst users = await userResponse.json()\n</code></pre>\n<p>JS waits for <code>/todos</code> to finish before starting <code>/users</code>.<br />\nIts like <strong><code>todos request time + users request time</code></strong></p>\n<hr />\n<p>In the <code>Promise.all()</code></p>\n<pre><code>const [todosRes, usersRes] = await Promise.all([\n    fetch('https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos'),\n    fetch('https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users')\n]);\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Both requests are started immediately.</strong> JS does not download them itself. The network layer handles the HTTP requests asynchronously. The event loop continues running, and the await resumes only when both promises are resolved.</p>\n<p>Its like <strong><code>max(todos request time, users request time)</code></strong></p>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"moonwave99","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/1073758/moonwave99"},"score":0,"last_activity_date":1778145597,"creation_date":1778145597,"answer_id":79937478,"question_id":79937456,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79937456/how-can-fix-this-problem-from-json-placeholder-ullrich/79937478#79937478","body":"<p>Also observe that <code>response.json()</code> can (and should) be parallelized as well - you can do so with an helper function like:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-js prettyprint-override\"><code>async function fetchAPI(url) {\n  return fetch(url).then(response =&gt; response.json());\n}\n\nasync function getTodos() {\n  const [todos, users] = await Promise.all([\n    'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos',\n    'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users'\n  ].map(fetchAPI));\n\n  // more idiomatic than using forEach\n  return todos.map(t =&gt; ({\n    ...t,\n    user: users.find(u =&gt; u.id === t.userId)\n  }));\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Viel Spaß!</p>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"UrbanwarfareStudios","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/4999894/urbanwarfarestudios"},"score":0,"last_activity_date":1777388733,"last_edit_date":1777388733,"creation_date":1777388391,"answer_id":79933146,"question_id":14342355,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/14342355/convert-tiff-to-jpg-in-php/79933146#79933146","body":"<p>I've recently gone through some trial and error with Tif to PNG using Imagick. I found some code which mentioned that tif files contain multipe images  -<a href=\"https://www.php.net/manual/en/imagick.getnumberimages.php\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"> you can use this function</a></p>\n<pre class=\"lang-php prettyprint-override\"><code>$image-&gt;getNumberImages()\n</code></pre>\n<p>I've found that all the Tif's I tested with had 2 images and the 2nd one gives the detail you want.</p>\n<pre><code>$final_path = str_replace($pathinfo['extension'],&quot;png&quot;,$base_path.$new_mock_path);\n\nswitch($pathinfo['extension']){\n  case 'tif':\n  case 'tiff':\n\n    $image = new \\Imagick($original_path);\n    $count = $image-&gt;getNumberImages();\n\n    for ($x = 1;$x &lt;= $image-&gt;getNumberImages(); $x++) {\n\n      if($x==2) {\n        // Save the 2nd image as PNG\n        $image-&gt;thumbnailImage(600, 0);             // keep aspect ratio\n        $image-&gt;setImageFormat('png');              // or 'jpeg'\n        $image-&gt;setImageCompressionQuality(70);     // JPEG quality\n        $image-&gt;writeImage($final_path);     // or .jpg$image-&gt;clear();\n          \n      }\n      // Save the PNG as a JPG\n      $img = new \\Imagick();\n      $img-&gt;readImage(''.$final_path.'');    // read first page only\n      $img-&gt;autoOrient();                       // fix EXIF orientation\n      $img-&gt;thumbnailImage(600, 0);             // keep aspect ratio\n      $img-&gt;setImageFormat('jpg');              // or 'jpeg'\n      $img-&gt;setImageCompressionQuality(70);     // JPEG quality\n\n      $image_filehandle = fopen(str_replace(&quot;.png&quot;,&quot;.jpg&quot;,$final_path), 'w+');\n\n      $img-&gt;writeImageFile($image_filehandle);     // or .jpg\n      $img-&gt;clear();\n  break;\n  default:\n\n    $img = new \\Imagick();\n    $img-&gt;readImage(''.$original_path.'');    // read first page only\n    $img-&gt;autoOrient();                       // fix EXIF orientation\n    $img-&gt;thumbnailImage(600, 0);             // keep aspect ratio\n    $img-&gt;setImageFormat('png');              // or 'jpeg'\n    $img-&gt;setImageCompressionQuality(70);     // JPEG quality\n    $image_filehandle = fopen($final_path, 'w+');\n\n    $img-&gt;writeImageFile($image_filehandle);     // or .jpg\n    $img-&gt;clear();\n  break;\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>I also discovered that if you want to convert TIF to JPG - the results are poor you lose a lot of detail going straight from tif &gt; jpg.</p>\n<p>You can great results if you go from tif &gt; png &gt; jpg.</p>\n<p>Hope this helps someone else</p>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"Martin Mohnhaupt","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/2061183/martin-mohnhaupt"},"score":0,"last_activity_date":1777219578,"last_edit_date":1777219578,"creation_date":1777217655,"answer_id":79932100,"question_id":42406520,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/42406520/populate-an-html-form-from-a-formdata-object/79932100#79932100","body":"<p>Since I've been working on this issue for the past few days, I tried implementing <strong>Sphinxxx</strong>'s solution.</p>\n<p>Unfortunately, in the  <code>formSerialize(form) {...}</code> function, ‘<strong>checkbox</strong>’ fields aren't exported if their value isn't ‘checked’. See <strong>Sphinxxx</strong>'s warnings !</p>\n<p>So I modified the code as follows:</p>\n<pre><code>    serialize_form(form) {\n        const result = [];\n        form.querySelectorAll(&quot;[name]&quot;).forEach((e) =&gt; {\n            switch (e.type) {\n                case &quot;checkbox&quot;:\n                    result.push(`${e.name}=${e.checked}`);\n                    break;\n                default:\n                    result.push(`${e.name}=${e.value}`);\n                    break;\n            }\n        });\n        return result.join(&quot;&amp;&quot;);\n    }\n\n    deserialize_form(form, serialized_form_data) {\n        const entries = new URLSearchParams(serialized_form_data).entries();\n        for (const [key, val] of entries) {\n            const input = form.elements[key];\n            switch (input.type) {\n                case &quot;checkbox&quot;:\n                    input.checked = !val;\n                    break;\n                default:\n                    input.value = val;\n                    break;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n</code></pre>\n<p>Kind regards to all,</p>\n<p>Martin</p>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"AlienWithPizza","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/9360315/alienwithpizza"},"score":5,"last_activity_date":1776274466,"last_edit_date":1776274466,"creation_date":1776135295,"answer_id":79925091,"question_id":79925071,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79925071/javascript-regular-expression-behaviour/79925091#79925091","body":"<blockquote>\n<ol>\n<li>Why are the <code>=</code> and <code>&amp;</code> substrings not matched as the splitter part, and therefore fall to the array?</li>\n</ol>\n</blockquote>\n<p>They are inside capture groups, which are included in the resulting array. <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/String/split#return_value\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Reference</a></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>If <code>separator</code> is a regex, the returned <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>Array</code></a> also contains the <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Regular_expressions/Capturing_group\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">captured groups</a> for each separator match; see below for details.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<blockquote>\n<ol start=\"2\">\n<li>Why are there two empty substrings at the array beginning?</li>\n</ol>\n</blockquote>\n<p><a href=\"https://regexr.com/8lkpf\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Example</a> Same as above, the first split to match on is '^abc=', the left side is empty string, the first capture is '^', so empty string, the second capture is '=', and then the right half of that split is 'def'.</p>\n<hr />\n<blockquote>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li>How can I get the expected output?</li>\n</ol>\n</blockquote>\n<ol>\n<li><p>Without modifying the regex, just skip those known indices.</p>\n<pre><code>&quot;abc=def&amp;ghi=jkl&amp;mno=pqr&quot;.split(/(^|&amp;).*?(=|$)/).filter((x,i) =&gt; i &amp;&amp; i%3===0)\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p>Make the regex groups non-capturing and skip the match before &quot;^&quot;.</p>\n<pre><code>&quot;abc=def&amp;ghi=jkl&amp;mno=pqr&quot;.split(/(?:^|&amp;).*?(?:=|$)/).slice(1)\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p>If you truly can make all the assumptions you have already made, perform the split in a multi step</p>\n<pre><code>&quot;abc=def&amp;ghi=jkl&amp;mno=pqr&quot;.split('&amp;').map(x =&gt; x.split('=')[1])\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p>Instead of trying to split, just a straight regex match with positive look behind/aheads (regexr.com does warn about lack of support in some browsers, but seems to work fine in the majors)</p>\n<pre><code>&quot;abc=def&amp;ghi=jkl&amp;mno=pqr&quot;.match(/(?&lt;==).*?(?=&amp;|$)/g)\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p>Use <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">URLSearchParams</a></p>\n<p>Not sure if this is related to what you're really trying to do, but there is a built in for working with query strings.</p>\n<pre><code>var params = new URLSearchParams(&quot;abc=def&amp;ghi=jkl&amp;mno=pqr&quot;);\nArray.from(params.keys()).join(','); // 'abc,ghi,mno'\nArray.from(params.values()).join(','); // 'def,jkl,pqr'\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n</ol>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"Roko C. Buljan","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/383904/roko-c-buljan"},"score":0,"last_activity_date":1776071908,"creation_date":1776071908,"answer_id":79924681,"question_id":79924199,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79924199/asynchronous-request-and-insertion-of-received-nodes/79924681#79924681","body":"<p>Basically you want to be able to create a <strong>new Element</strong> via some <code>elNew</code> function:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-js prettyprint-override\"><code>elNew(&quot;div&quot;, { className: &quot;header&quot; }, ...ExtendedDynamicChildrenOptions);\n</code></pre>\n<p>without necessarily having to store the above into i.e: <code>this.elHeader = </code> and that accepts additional options to go fetch children elements and append them once loaded.</p>\n<p>If we start with this two utility functions:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-js prettyprint-override\"><code>// Utils\nconst el = (sel, par = document) =&gt; par.querySelector(sel);\nconst elNew = (tag, prop) =&gt; Object.assign(document.createElement(tag), prop);\n</code></pre>\n<p>you'll pretty quickly understand that what you actually need a <code>elNewExt</code> - capable of fetching and creating children from another resource - basically <em><strong>extending</strong></em> the <code>elNew</code> functionality:</p>\n<p><div class=\"snippet\" data-lang=\"js\" data-hide=\"false\" data-console=\"true\" data-babel=\"false\" data-babel-preset-react=\"false\" data-babel-preset-ts=\"false\">\n<div class=\"snippet-code\">\n<pre class=\"snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override\"><code>// Utils\nconst el = (sel, par = document) =&gt; par.querySelector(sel);\nconst elNew = (tag, prop) =&gt; Object.assign(document.createElement(tag), prop);\nconst elNewExt = async (tag, prop, opt) =&gt; {\n  const el = elNew(tag, prop);\n  opt.parent?.append(el);\n  try {\n    const res = await fetch(opt.url, {method: opt.method ?? \"GET\", body: JSON.stringify(opt.data), headers: {'Content-Type': 'application/json'}});\n    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`Request for children data failed with status ${res.status}`);\n    const data = (await res.json()).slice(opt.from, opt.to);\n    el.innerHTML = \"\"; // Clear existing content\n    el.append(...data.map(opt.children));\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.error(err.message);\n  }\n  return el;\n};\n\n// App\nconst app = {\n  init() {\n    elNewExt(\"div\", {\n      className: \"header\",\n      textContent: \"Loading...\"\n    }, /* Ext options: */ {\n      url: \"https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts\",\n      to: 3, // get first N items\n      children: (ch, i) =&gt; elNew(\"div\", { className: \"child\", textContent: `${i+1}: ${ch.title}` }),\n      parent: el(\"body\"),\n    });\n  }\n};\n\n// Launch\napp.init();</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override\"><code>.child { outline: 1px solid red; }</code></pre>\n</div>\n</div>\n</p>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"Naren Murali","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/5924562/naren-murali"},"score":0,"last_activity_date":1775735027,"creation_date":1775735027,"answer_id":79922794,"question_id":79922724,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79922724/should-i-use-null-or-undefined-as-a-default-starting-value-for-my-signal/79922794#79922794","body":"<p>No major difference between <code>null</code> and <code>undefined</code> with signals as far as I am aware.</p>\n<p>When you return <code>undefined</code> as a return value of <code>params</code> of any of the Angular Resource API.</p>\n<p>The API call is not made, until a proper value is defined, which is one scenario, where <code>undefined</code> is preferential.</p>\n<p>Below is an example, highlighting the similarities and resource API behaviour.</p>\n<pre><code>import {\n  Component,\n  signal,\n  computed,\n  model,\n  linkedSignal,\n  resource,\n} from '@angular/core';\nimport { rxResource } from '@angular/core/rxjs-interop';\nimport { bootstrapApplication } from '@angular/platform-browser';\n\n@Component({\n  selector: 'app-root',\n  template: `\n  &lt;h1&gt;Undefined&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;hr/&gt;\n  &lt;h5&gt;using computed&lt;/h5&gt;\n  &lt;div&gt;{{undefinedComputed()}}&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;h5&gt;using linkedSignal&lt;/h5&gt;\n  &lt;div&gt;{{undefinedLinkedSignal()}}&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;h5&gt;using resource API&lt;/h5&gt;\n  &lt;div&gt;{{undefinedResource.value()}}&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;null&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;hr/&gt;\n  &lt;h5&gt;using computed&lt;/h5&gt;\n  &lt;div&gt;{{nullComputed()}}&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;h5&gt;using linkedSignal&lt;/h5&gt;\n  &lt;div&gt;{{nullLinkedSignal()}}&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;h5&gt;using resource API&lt;/h5&gt;\n  &lt;div&gt;{{nullResource.value()}}&lt;/div&gt;\n  `,\n})\nexport class App {\n  undefinedProp = signal&lt;string | undefined&gt;(undefined);\n  nullProp = signal&lt;string | null&gt;(null);\n\n  undefinedComputed = computed(() =&gt; `asdf123${this.undefinedProp()}`);\n  undefinedLinkedSignal = linkedSignal({\n    source: () =&gt; this.undefinedProp(),\n    computation: () =&gt; `asdf123`,\n  });\n  undefinedResource = resource({\n    params: () =&gt; this.undefinedProp(),\n    stream: async () =&gt; {\n      const response = await fetch(\n        'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos/1'\n      );\n      return await response.json(); // 2. Parse JSON\n    },\n  });\n\n  nullComputed = computed(() =&gt; `asdf123${this.nullProp()}`);\n  nullLinkedSignal = linkedSignal({\n    source: () =&gt; this.nullProp(),\n    computation: () =&gt; `asdf123`,\n  });\n  nullResource = resource({\n    params: () =&gt; this.nullProp() || undefined,\n    stream: async ({}) =&gt; {\n      const response = await fetch(\n        'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos/1'\n      );\n      return await response.json(); // 2. Parse JSON\n    },\n  });\n}\n\nbootstrapApplication(App);\n</code></pre>\n<h3><a href=\"https://stackblitz.com/edit/stackblitz-starters-znazynwg\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Stackblitz Demo</a></h3>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"gene b.","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/1005607/gene-b"},"score":0,"last_activity_date":1775567463,"creation_date":1775567463,"answer_id":79921564,"question_id":79920981,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79920981/next-js-automatic-redirect-of-legacy-language-enabled-url-es-to-core-pag/79921564#79921564","body":"<p>The only way I found to do it is to redirect to a temporary <code>?lang=..</code> URL using <code>next.config.ts</code>'s <code>redirects</code> section, and then redirect one more time to a clean URL without the <code>?lang=..</code> param in the component. It's similar to before but at least there's no blank page flickering anymore, it's the content flickering, which is a little better.</p>\n<pre><code>const nextConfig: NextConfig = {\n  async redirects() {\n      return [\n        {\n          source: '/page1-es',\n          destination: '/page1?lang=es',\n          permanent: true,\n        },       \n        /* etc. */\n</code></pre>\n<p>then in the component,</p>\n<pre><code>const pathname = usePathname();\n\nuseEffect(() =&gt; {\n    const searchParams = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);\n    let langParam = searchParams.get(&quot;lang&quot;);\n    if (langParam) {\n        i18n.changeLanguage(langParam);\n        redirect(pathname); // Redirect to the respective clean URL without the &quot;?lang=..&quot; URL param after processing\n    }\n}, []);\n</code></pre>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"Naren Murali","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/5924562/naren-murali"},"score":0,"last_activity_date":1772528824,"creation_date":1772528824,"answer_id":79899993,"question_id":79899937,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79899937/avoid-initial-request-on-rxresource-creation/79899993#79899993","body":"<p>If you just want to make the API call without any signals being involved, you need to set the resource to a <code>local</code> value using the <code>set</code> method on the <code>constructor</code>, then use <code>reload</code> to trigger the API.</p>\n<p>Ideally there should be a flag or something, we can use to prevent the initial load. As on 03-03-2026 this is one possible way. Maybe it gets added in the future.</p>\n<pre><code>export class AppComponent {\n  httpClient = inject(HttpClient);\n  id = signal&lt;any&gt;({ id: undefined });\n  resourceFetchedAlways = rxResource({\n    // if using angular 19 less or equal versions\n    // replace params, with request and stream with loader.\n    stream: () =&gt; {\n      return this.httpClient.get(`https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos/`);\n    },\n  });\n\n  constructor() {\n    this.resourceFetchedAlways.set([]);\n  }\n\n  ngOnInit() {\n    setTimeout(() =&gt; {\n      this.resourceFetchedAlways.reload();\n    }, 3000);\n  }\n}\n</code></pre>\n<h3><a href=\"https://stackblitz.com/edit/angular-21-base-wdgjtgcb?file=src%2Fapp%2Fapp.component.ts\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Stackblitz Demo</a></h3>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"Naren Murali","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/5924562/naren-murali"},"score":2,"last_activity_date":1772525078,"last_edit_date":1772525078,"creation_date":1772524769,"answer_id":79899950,"question_id":79899937,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79899937/avoid-initial-request-on-rxresource-creation/79899950#79899950","body":"<p>Yes we simply need to return <code>undefined</code> on the <code>params</code> (Greater than Angular 19) or <code>request</code> (Older than or equal to Angular 19). Until the required <code>signals</code> are properly set to make the API call.</p>\n<hr />\n<h5>Fix: Return <code>undefined</code> to prevent the API to be triggered until ready.</h5>\n<h4>TS:</h4>\n<pre><code>export class AppComponent {\n  httpClient = inject(HttpClient);\n  id = signal&lt;any&gt;({ id: undefined });\n  resourceFetchedAlways = rxResource({\n    // if using angular 19 less or equal versions\n    // replace params, with request and stream with loader.\n    // you can just do this.id()?.id for simplicity\n    params: () =&gt; {\n      const idData = this.id();\n      // here api is not called unless id value is present\n      if(idData?.id) {\n        return idData;\n      }\n      // return undefined to prevent API call trigger\n      return undefined;\n    },\n    stream: ({ params: { id } }) =&gt; {\n      return this.httpClient.get(\n        `https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos/${id}`\n      );\n    },\n  });\n\n  ngOnInit() {\n    setTimeout(() =&gt; {\n      this.id.set({ id: 2 });\n    }, 3000);\n  }\n}\n</code></pre>\n<h4>HTML:</h4>\n<pre><code>&lt;p&gt;Angular 21 Works!&lt;/p&gt;\n@let status = resourceFetchedAlways.status(); \n@if(status === 'idle') { \n  Resource API is idle \n} @if(status === 'error') { \n  Error! \n} @if(status === 'resolved') {\n  {{ resourceFetchedAlways.value() | json }}\n}\n</code></pre>\n<h3><a href=\"https://stackblitz.com/edit/angular-21-base-s6cyzgzx\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Stackblitz Demo</a></h3>\n<hr />\n<h5>Issue Demo: Without returning <code>undefined</code> always triggers the API on initialization (Error).</h5>\n<pre><code>export class AppComponent {\n  httpClient = inject(HttpClient);\n  id = signal({ id: undefined });\n  resourceFetchedAlways = rxResource({\n    params: () =&gt; this.id(),\n    stream: ({ params: { id } }) =&gt; {\n      return this.httpClient.get(\n        `https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos/${id}`\n      );\n    },\n  });\n}\n</code></pre>\n<h3><a href=\"https://stackblitz.com/edit/angular-21-base-26a6p7ag\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Stackblitz Demo</a></h3>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"sthames42","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/2245849/sthames42"},"score":0,"last_activity_date":1772157056,"creation_date":1772157056,"answer_id":79897467,"question_id":74024709,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/74024709/ansible-configuration-paths-not-relative-to-ansible-cfg/79897467#79897467","body":"<h2>TL;DR</h2>\n<p>AFAICT, all config file settings that resolve to a path are relative to the\nconfiguration file except those found in <code>vault_identity_list</code>. However, all\npath settings may include embedded environment variables.</p>\n<p>Given <code>ansible.cfg</code> and vault password file(s) live in <code>~/myproject/conf</code>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Set an environment variable with the config file directory:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-bash prettyprint-override\"><code>export ANSIBLE_CONFIG=&quot;~/myproject/conf/ansible.cfg&quot;\nexport CONFIG_PATH=&quot;$(dirname &quot;$ANSIBLE_CONFIG&quot;)&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>or</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-bash prettyprint-override\"><code>CONFIG_FILE=&quot;$(ansible-config dump | sed -n 's/CONFIG_FILE() = //p')&quot;\nexport CONFIG_PATH=&quot;$(dirname &quot;$CONFIG_FILE&quot;)&quot;\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p>And embed the variable in the <code>vault-id</code> path(s) of <code>vault_identity_list</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-ini prettyprint-override\"><code>vault_identity_list    = latest@$CONFIG_PATH/vault_password, \n                         older@$CONFIG_PATH/vault_password-older,\n                         oldest@$CONFIG_PATH/vault_password-oldest\nvault_encrypt_identity = latest\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Path Settings</h2>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.ansible.com/projects/ansible/latest/reference_appendices/config.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Configuration setting</a> specifications are found in <a href=\"https://github.com/ansible/ansible/blob/4f1576ae43f0524ab632e7ab9c746db70a5bd1d5/lib/ansible/config/base.yml\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>base.yml</code></a> which is\nread by <a href=\"https://github.com/ansible/ansible/blob/4f1576ae43f0524ab632e7ab9c746db70a5bd1d5/lib/ansible/config/manager.py#L343\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>lib/ansible/config/manager.py</code></a> to populate the configuration\nsettings before execution. For each setting, specifications include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>type</code></strong> - type describing how the setting should be interpreted,</li>\n<li><strong><code>ini</code></strong>  - configuration file section and key,</li>\n<li><strong><code>env</code></strong>  - environment variable(s) that may contain the setting, and</li>\n<li><strong><code>vars</code></strong> - Ansible variable(s) that may contain the setting.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When a setting of type <code>path</code>, <code>pathlist</code>, or <code>pathspec</code> is loaded from an\nenvironment variable or Ansible variable, the path(s) are resolved relative to\nthe current working directory.</p>\n<p>When loaded from a configuration file, <code>manager.py</code> passes the configuration\nfile directory as <code>origin</code> to the <a href=\"https://github.com/ansible/ansible/blob/4f1576ae43f0524ab632e7ab9c746db70a5bd1d5/lib/ansible/config/manager.py#L76\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>ensure_type()</code> function</a> and path(s)\nare resolved relative to <code>origin</code>.</p>\n<p>For example, in <code>base.yml</code>, the <code>ini</code> key of the <code>DEFAULT_VAULT_PASSWORD_FILE</code>\nsetting is <code>vault_password_file</code> and the <code>type</code> value is <code>path</code>. So, a relative\nfile path is resolved to the configuration file directory.</p>\n<h2>The Problem</h2>\n<p>The configuration setting <code>DEFAULT_VAULT_IDENTITY_LIST</code>, with the <code>ini</code> key of\n<code>vault_identity_list</code>, has a <code>type</code> value of <code>list</code>. So, file paths are not\nresolved when the config file is read. Whether loaded from a\nconfiguration file or environment variable, <code>DEFAULT_VAULT_IDENTITY_LIST</code> will\nbe a list of Vault IDs in the format of <code>label@source</code>, <a href=\"https://docs.ansible.com/projects/ansible/latest/vault_guide/vault_managing_passwords.html#managing-multiple-passwords-with-vault-ids\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">where <code>source</code> may be\nan unresolved file path</a>.</p>\n<p>The base for all Ansible commands is the <a href=\"https://github.com/ansible/ansible/blob/4f1576ae43f0524ab632e7ab9c746db70a5bd1d5/lib/ansible/cli/__init__.py#L552\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>ansible.cli</code> module</a>, which\ncomplements <code>DEFAULT_VAULT_IDENTITY_LIST</code> with any <code>vault-id</code> command-line\noptions. For each Vault ID, it calls <a href=\"https://github.com/ansible/ansible/blob/e82d9564f0592e1ca48403d9b4d2cee7871ef4f2/lib/ansible/parsing/vault/__init__.py#L352\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>ansible.parsing.vault</code></a> to read the\npassword from <code>source</code>. Unfortunately, the origin of <code>DEFAULT_VAULT_IDENTITY_LIST</code>,\nbe it from the configuration file or environment variable, is not known by\n<code>vault</code> so it resolves relative paths from the current working directory.</p>\n<h2>The Workaround</h2>\n<p>While it doesn't appear to be well documented, all path configuration settings\nget resolved in <a href=\"https://github.com/ansible/ansible/blob/4f1576ae43f0524ab632e7ab9c746db70a5bd1d5/lib/ansible/utils/path.py#L29\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>ansible.utils.path.unfrackpath()</code></a>, including Vault ID\nsource. <code>unfrackpath()</code> resolves the user home directory (~) and any embedded\nenvironment variables, allowing for the workaround described, above.</p>\n<h2>The Solution</h2>\n<p>Since the only time it is known if a configuration setting comes from the\nconfig file is when the file is read, a new setting <code>type</code> should be added to\n<a href=\"https://github.com/ansible/ansible/blob/4f1576ae43f0524ab632e7ab9c746db70a5bd1d5/lib/ansible/config/manager.py#L122\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>lib/ansible/config/manager/_ensure_type()</code></a> to resolve Vault ID source\npaths from <code>vault_identity_list</code> the same way other path settings in the config\nfile are resolved. Changing the <code>type</code> for <code>DEFAULT_VAULT_IDENTITY_LIST</code> in\n<code>base.yml</code> to the new type would then fix the issue.</p>\n<p>Here is a working example of the needed code change in <code>_ensure_type()</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-py prettyprint-override\"><code>case 'vaultidlist':\n    if isinstance(value, str):\n        value = [x.strip() for x in value.split(',')]\n\n    if isinstance(value, Sequence) and not isinstance(value, bytes) and all(isinstance(x, str) for x in value):\n        return [\n            '@'.join([ label, resolve_path(source, basedir=basedir) ]) \n                if source and source not in ['prompt', 'prompt_ask_vault_pass'] \n                else f&quot;{label}@{source}&quot; if source \n                else label\n            for label, source in [\n                id.split('@') if '@' in id else [id, None] \n                for id in value\n            ]\n        ]\n</code></pre>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"Pavlo Hushuliak","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/30642015/pavlo-hushuliak"},"score":2,"last_activity_date":1772050278,"creation_date":1772050278,"answer_id":79896508,"question_id":79896487,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79896487/microsoft-calendar-authorization-expiration-issue/79896508#79896508","body":"<pre><code>import { Client } from &quot;@microsoft/microsoft-graph-client&quot;;\n\nimport type {\n  CalendarProvider,\n  OAuthTokens,\n  Calendar,\n  ConnectionTestResult,\n  UserInfo,\n  Contact,\n  CreateEventParams,\n  UpdateEventParams,\n  DeleteEventParams,\n  SearchEventsParams,\n  CreatedEvent\n} from &quot;../types&quot;;\nimport {\n  oauthTokensSchema,\n  calendarSchema,\n  microsoftCalendarListSchema,\n  microsoftCalendarSchema,\n  connectionTestResultSchema\n} from &quot;../types&quot;;\nimport { MICROSOFT_OAUTH_CONFIG, OAUTH_ENDPOINTS } from &quot;../oauth&quot;;\n\nexport class MicrosoftCalendarProvider implements CalendarProvider {\n\n  constructor() {}\n\n  getAuthUrl(redirectUri: string, state?: string): string {\n    const params = new URLSearchParams({\n      client_id: MICROSOFT_OAUTH_CONFIG.clientId,\n      response_type: &quot;code&quot;,\n      redirect_uri: redirectUri,\n      scope: MICROSOFT_OAUTH_CONFIG.scopes.join(&quot; &quot;),\n      response_mode: &quot;query&quot;,\n      prompt: &quot;consent&quot;, // Force consent to always return a refresh token\n      ...(state &amp;&amp; { state }),\n    });\n\n    return `${OAUTH_ENDPOINTS.microsoft.auth}?${params.toString()}`;\n  }\n\n  async exchangeCodeForTokens(code: string, redirectUri: string): Promise&lt;OAuthTokens&gt; {\n    try {\n      // Use direct OAuth2 token endpoint instead of MSAL\n      // This gives us the actual refresh_token string (MSAL hides it in its internal cache)\n      const body = new URLSearchParams({\n        client_id: MICROSOFT_OAUTH_CONFIG.clientId,\n        client_secret: MICROSOFT_OAUTH_CONFIG.clientSecret,\n        code,\n        redirect_uri: redirectUri,\n        grant_type: 'authorization_code',\n        scope: MICROSOFT_OAUTH_CONFIG.scopes.join(' '),\n      });\n\n      const response = await fetch(OAUTH_ENDPOINTS.microsoft.token, {\n        method: 'POST',\n        headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded' },\n        body: body.toString(),\n      });\n\n      if (!response.ok) {\n        const errorData = await response.json().catch(() =&gt; ({}));\n        throw new Error(`Token exchange failed: ${response.status} ${JSON.stringify(errorData)}`);\n      }\n\n      const data = await response.json();\n\n      if (!data.access_token) {\n        throw new Error(&quot;No access token received from Microsoft&quot;);\n      }\n\n      const expiresAt = new Date();\n      if (data.expires_in) {\n        expiresAt.setSeconds(expiresAt.getSeconds() + data.expires_in);\n      } else {\n        // Default to 1 hour if no expiry provided\n        expiresAt.setHours(expiresAt.getHours() + 1);\n      }\n\n      console.log('Microsoft token exchange successful', {\n        hasAccessToken: !!data.access_token,\n        hasRefreshToken: !!data.refresh_token,\n        expiresIn: data.expires_in,\n        scope: data.scope,\n      });\n\n      const tokenData = {\n        accessToken: data.access_token,\n        refreshToken: data.refresh_token || undefined, // Real refresh token from OAuth response\n        expiresAt,\n        scope: data.scope || undefined,\n      };\n\n      return oauthTokensSchema.parse(tokenData);\n    } catch (error) {\n      throw new Error(`Microsoft OAuth token exchange failed: ${error}`);\n    }\n  }\n\n  async getUserInfo(accessToken: string): Promise&lt;UserInfo&gt; {\n    try {\n      const graphClient = Client.init({\n        authProvider: (done) =&gt; {\n          done(null, accessToken);\n        },\n      });\n\n      const userInfo = await graphClient\n        .api(&quot;/me&quot;)\n        .select(&quot;id,mail,userPrincipalName,displayName&quot;)\n        .get();\n\n      // Microsoft may use either 'mail' or 'userPrincipalName' for email\n      const email = userInfo.mail || userInfo.userPrincipalName;\n\n      if (!email || !userInfo.id) {\n        throw new Error(&quot;Required user information not available from Microsoft&quot;);\n      }\n\n      return {\n        email: email,\n        name: userInfo.displayName || undefined,\n        id: userInfo.id,\n      };\n    } catch (error: any) {\n      throw new Error(`Failed to fetch Microsoft user info: ${error.message}`);\n    }\n  }\n\n  async refreshTokens(refreshToken: string): Promise&lt;OAuthTokens&gt; {\n    try {\n      // Use direct OAuth2 token endpoint for refresh\n      const body = new URLSearchParams({\n        client_id: MICROSOFT_OAUTH_CONFIG.clientId,\n        client_secret: MICROSOFT_OAUTH_CONFIG.clientSecret,\n        refresh_token: refreshToken,\n        grant_type: 'refresh_token',\n        scope: MICROSOFT_OAUTH_CONFIG.scopes.join(' '),\n      });\n\n      const response = await fetch(OAUTH_ENDPOINTS.microsoft.token, {\n        method: 'POST',\n        headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded' },\n        body: body.toString(),\n      });\n\n      if (!response.ok) {\n        const errorData = await response.json().catch(() =&gt; ({}));\n        throw new Error(`Token refresh failed: ${response.status} ${JSON.stringify(errorData)}`);\n      }\n\n      const data = await response.json();\n\n      if (!data.access_token) {\n        throw new Error(&quot;No access token received from Microsoft refresh&quot;);\n      }\n\n      const expiresAt = new Date();\n      if (data.expires_in) {\n        expiresAt.setSeconds(expiresAt.getSeconds() + data.expires_in);\n      } else {\n        expiresAt.setHours(expiresAt.getHours() + 1);\n      }\n\n      console.log('Microsoft token refresh successful', {\n        hasNewAccessToken: !!data.access_token,\n        hasNewRefreshToken: !!data.refresh_token,\n        expiresIn: data.expires_in,\n      });\n\n      const tokenData = {\n        accessToken: data.access_token,\n        // Microsoft may rotate refresh tokens - use new one if provided, keep old otherwise\n        refreshToken: data.refresh_token || refreshToken,\n        expiresAt,\n        scope: data.scope || undefined,\n      };\n\n      return oauthTokensSchema.parse(tokenData);\n    } catch (error) {\n      throw new Error(`Microsoft token refresh failed: ${error}`);\n    }\n  }\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here is full solution.</p>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"Jesse Johnson","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/540756/jesse-johnson"},"score":0,"last_activity_date":1771880535,"creation_date":1771880535,"answer_id":79894958,"question_id":79891548,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79891548/hi-i-m-using-puppeteer-connected-to-a-self-hosted-browserless-instance-via-webs/79894958#79894958","body":"<p>My guess is that your proxy credentials include special characters that are failing because they aren’t being URL encoded properly, or that using both individual query parameters and the JSON launch parameter is causing a conflict.</p>\n<p>Try using the following snippet to generate the connection URL and connect via Puppeteer:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-js prettyprint-override\"><code>const TOKEN = &quot;xx12345xx&quot;;\nconst proxyUrl = encodeURIComponent(&quot;http://USERNAME:PASSWORD@p.webshare.io:80&quot;);\n\nconst launchOptions = {\n  headless: false,\n  stealth: true,\n  blockConsentModals: true,\n  externalProxyServer: proxyUrl\n};\n\nconst queryParams = new URLSearchParams({\n  token: TOKEN,\n  launch: JSON.stringify(launchOptions)\n});\n\nconst browser = await puppeteer.connect({\n  browserWSEndpoint: `wss://brows.harshchaudhary.dev/chromium?${queryParams.toString()}`,\n});\n</code></pre>\n<p>Links to relevant documentation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><a href=\"https://docs.browserless.io/baas/features/proxies#third-party-proxies\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Third Party Proxies</a></p>\n</li>\n<li><p><a href=\"https://docs.browserless.io/baas/launch-options\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Launch Options</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"Richard Milton","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/32403925/richard-milton"},"score":1,"last_activity_date":1771843606,"creation_date":1771843606,"answer_id":79894523,"question_id":79672554,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79672554/mailgun-sending-api-works-on-local-dev-server-but-catches-bad-request-error-on/79894523#79894523","body":"<p>Thanks for the heads up was quite tricky to figure out using the Cloudflare fetch (not the same as browser fetch) here's my code that worked (the <code>getEnv()</code> is my own utility wrapper for <code>getCloudflareContext</code>). Hope it helps someone</p>\n<pre><code>import { getEnv } from &quot;@/util&quot;\n\ntype MessageData = {\n  to: string[]\n  subject: string\n  text: string\n}\n\nexport const sendMail = async (data: MessageData) =&gt; {\n  const env = await getEnv()\n\n  const formData = new URLSearchParams()\n  formData.append('subject', data.subject)\n  formData.append('text', data.text)\n  formData.append('to', data.to.reduce((prev, curr) =&gt; `${prev},${curr}`))\n  formData.append('from', env.EMAIL_SEND_FROM)\n\n  const requestOptions = {\n    method: &quot;POST&quot;,\n    headers: {\n      Authorization: 'Basic ' + Buffer.from('api:' + env.EMAIL_SEND_KEY).toString('base64')\n    },\n    body: formData,\n  }\n\n  try {\n    const resp = await fetch(`${env.EMAIL_SEND_API}/v3/${env.EMAIL_SEND_DOM}/messages`, requestOptions)\n\n    console.log(await resp.json())\n  } catch (error) {\n    console.error(error)\n  }\n}\n</code></pre>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"Violet Rose","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/3984972/violet-rose"},"score":0,"last_activity_date":1771017343,"last_edit_date":1771017343,"creation_date":1771015496,"answer_id":79889011,"question_id":71480469,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/71480469/should-i-use-iife-in-useeffect-hook/79889011#79889011","body":"<p>The use of an async IIFE is common and great to avoid &quot;callback hell&quot;.</p>\n<p>However, when setting state in it and if it depends on some state, you should <a href=\"https://maxrozen.com/race-conditions-fetching-data-react-with-useeffect\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">always avoid race conditions by using a cleanup function</a>.</p>\n<p>By checking the flag at the end of the IIFE before <strong>synchronously</strong> setting all of your states, you ensure that you are not override a future call of the effect that happened to finish before you. It is important that all of your state changes ocur in one syncronous block, otherwise the flag could change between them (a time-of-check-time-of-use bug).</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-js prettyprint-override\"><code>useEffect(() =&gt; {\n  // flag to set at cleanup\n  let cancelled = false;\n  (async () =&gt; {\n    const response = await fetch(\n      &quot;https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts?state=&quot; + statefulValue\n    );\n    const json = await response.json();\n    // throw away the changes if we've been cleaned up\n    if (!cancelled) {\n      setPosts(json);\n    }\n  })();\n  // react will call this cleanup funciton when the component unmounts and whenever the effect is called again due to a dependent state change.\n  return () =&gt; {\n    cancelled = true;\n  }\n  // add dependency array for example\n}, [statefulValue]);\n</code></pre>\n<p>A more performant approach where supported is to use a <code>AbortController</code> to cancel the fetch early:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-js prettyprint-override\"><code>useEffect(() =&gt; {\n  // AbortController to abort at cleanup\n  const abortController = new AbortController();\n  (async () =&gt; {\n    try {\n      const response = await fetch(\n        &quot;https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts?state=&quot; + statefulValue,\n        {\n          // pass signal to fetch\n          signal: abortController.signal,\n        }\n      );\n      const json = await response.json();\n      // throw away the changes if we've been cleaned up\n      if (!abortController.signal.aborted) {\n        setPosts(json);\n      }\n    } catch (e) {\n      if (error.name === 'AbortError') {\n        // swallow abort errors\n      } else {\n        // rethrow others, examples: network errors, json parse error, etc.\n        // you could deal with these better if you'd want to.\n        throw e\n      }\n    }\n  })();\n  // react will call this cleanup funciton when the component unmounts and whenever the effect is called again due to a dependent state change.\n  return () =&gt; {\n    abortController.abort();\n  }\n  // add dependency array for example\n}, [statefulValue]);\n</code></pre>\n<p>Lastly, you could use a library that is implemented to handle all of this for you automatically, efficiently, and correctly. Beyond the pitfalls discussed above with race conditions, there are opportunities for caching in more complex applications where the same fetch request may be used in multiple places, or if you want to keep the data cached for a period of time based on response headers, etc.</p>\n<p>The most popular one as of 2026 is @tanstack/react-query, but other options exist as well:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://tanstack.com/query/latest\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">@tanstack/react-query</a> - popular, mature, featureful</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://swr.vercel.app/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">swr</a> - simple, small package, similar to tanstack query though not as featureful</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.apollographql.com/docs/react\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">@apollo/client</a> - large package, simpler ergonomics for graphql with &quot;normalized caching&quot; (meaning data responses from different requests can be cached for other requests based on the graph schema)</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://redux-toolkit.js.org/rtk-query/overview\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">rtk-query</a> - the go-to solution for redux</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://reactrouter.com/home\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">react-router</a> - full application router with built in data fetching/mutation (actions) ergonomics</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"jsbueno","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/108205/jsbueno"},"score":0,"last_activity_date":1769532207,"creation_date":1769532207,"answer_id":79877316,"question_id":79801530,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79801530/why-are-my-asyncio-http-requests-not-overlapping-as-expected-for-i-o/79877316#79877316","body":"<p>&quot;Works for me&quot;</p>\n<p>The very snippet you put there performs concurrently as expected - here is my output\nwith Python 3.12:</p>\n<pre><code>(env312) gwidion@fedora:~/...$ python xis.py\n[1769531835.54] Starting request for: https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos/1\n[1769531835.55] Starting request for: https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos/2\n[1769531835.55] Starting request for: https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1\n[1769531835.55] Starting request for: https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users/1\n[1769531835.55] Starting request for: https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/comments/1\n[1769531835.71] Finished request for: https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1 in 0.1582s\n[1769531835.71] Finished request for: https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/comments/1 in 0.1578s\n[1769531835.72] Finished request for: https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users/1 in 0.1650s\n[1769531835.72] Finished request for: https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos/1 in 0.1762s\n[1769531835.72] Finished request for: https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos/2 in 0.1667s\n\n[1769531835.72] All tasks completed in 0.1782 seconds.\n</code></pre>\n<p>If you are getting the same issues both with your own API and with the public server, these are being serialized in some other layer in your operating system. Maybe you have a firewall or antivirus, or VPN software which could be meddling with your requests and capping concurrent HTTP calls to the same domain?</p>\n<p>Ah, I see you raised the question yourself:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Could it be related to DNS resolution, TCP connection limits (either local or on the server), or something in my ClientSession setup?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>So, given the results obtained, I'd check your local TCP connection limits, from one of the possible sources above. DNS would not serialize the calls in this way, but otoh, it should be something easy for you to swap and try another one.</p>\n<p>(It also worked on Python 3.14, the output above is with 3.12.9).</p>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"Naren Murali","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/5924562/naren-murali"},"score":0,"last_activity_date":1769218348,"creation_date":1769218348,"answer_id":79874962,"question_id":79874891,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79874891/can-you-add-extra-item-to-data-retrieved-with-httpresource/79874962#79874962","body":"<p>It depends, on whether you want the original data to be unmodified and used elsewhere.</p>\n<h3>Keep original data and use <code>linkedSignal</code> for local changes:</h3>\n<p>If you have the scenario where you fetched the data using <code>httpResource</code> and want to preserve the original data. As well as use the data for manipulations.</p>\n<p>You can created a derived state using <code>linkedSignal</code> (Which supports signal updates as well as replaces the state when the source signal changes).</p>\n<p>Below is an example highlighting the usage of <code>linkedSignal</code> to create a clone of the original data, which can be used for data manipulations.</p>\n<p>When we click the refresh button, the source signal updates and the linkedSignal (Local changes) are discarded.</p>\n<h3>TS:</h3>\n<pre><code>// original data. need to reuse in a dropdown.\ndata = httpResource&lt;any&gt;(() =&gt; `https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos`, {\n  parse: (response: any) =&gt; {\n    return response.slice(0, 15);\n  },\n});\n// list data which can be modified.\ndataList = linkedSignal(() =&gt; {\n  if (this.data.status() === 'resolved') {\n    // or\n    // return JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(this.data.value());\n    return structuredClone(this.data.value());\n  }\n  return [];\n});\n\n// updates to the linked signal do not affect the main http Resource\nchangeTitle(index: any) {\n  this.dataList.update((dataList) =&gt; {\n    dataList[index].title = 'Changed ' + dataList[index].title;\n    return [...dataList];\n  });\n}\n</code></pre>\n<h3>HTML:</h3>\n<pre><code>&lt;button (click)=&quot;data.reload()&quot;&gt;refresh&lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;h3&gt;linkedSignal value can be modified when needed as a normal signal&lt;/h3&gt;\n@if(data.error()) {\n  Error when fetching data.\n} @else if (data.isLoading()) {\n  Loading...\n} @else {\n  @for(item of dataList();track $index) {\n    &lt;div&gt;#{{item.id}} - {{item.title}}&lt;/div&gt;&lt;button (click)=&quot;changeTitle($index)&quot;&gt; Change Title&lt;/button&gt;\n  }\n}\n&lt;h3&gt;Original data is reused without being influenced by the derived state (linkedSignal)&lt;/h3&gt;\n&lt;select&gt;\n  @for(item of data.value();track $index) {\n    &lt;option&gt;#{{item.id}} - {{item.title}}&lt;/option&gt;\n  }\n&lt;/select&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<h3><a href=\"https://stackblitz.com/edit/angular-dlyk11wa?file=src%2Fmain.ts\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Stackblitz Demo</a></h3>\n<hr />\n<h3>Allow <code>httpResource</code> data modifications (Source data need not be preserved):</h3>\n<p>When you just want to manipulate the data, we can directly use the <code>httpResource</code>'s <code>update</code> and <code>set</code> method to perform updates.</p>\n<pre><code>changeTitle(index: any) {\n  this.data.update((dataList) =&gt; {\n    dataList[index].title = 'Changed ' + dataList[index].title;\n    return [...dataList];\n  });\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Suppose you want to add an extra element during initialization. Then we can use the parse method to perform this initialization.</p>\n<pre><code>data = httpResource&lt;any&gt;(() =&gt; `https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos`, {\n  parse: (response: any) =&gt; {\n    // add a default element during initialization,\n    response.unshift({ id: '0', title: '&lt;&lt;Enter a value&gt;&gt;' });\n    return response.slice(0, 16);\n  },\n});\n</code></pre>\n<p>The most important thing to remember here is that. When the API is fetched and does not have any local changes, the resource status will always be <code>'resolved'</code>. But when the resource is updated with local changes, the resource status will be <code>'local'</code>.</p>\n<p>So be mindful of this when writing <code>@if</code> or <code>if</code> conditions using the resource API status signal (<code>this.data.status()</code>).</p>\n<h3>Full Code:</h3>\n<pre><code>import 'zone.js';\nimport { Component, linkedSignal, ResourceStatus } from '@angular/core';\nimport { bootstrapApplication } from '@angular/platform-browser';\nimport { httpResource, provideHttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';\nimport { JsonPipe } from '@angular/common';\n\n@Component({\n  selector: 'app-root',\n  template: `\n    &lt;h3&gt;linkedSignal value can be modified when needed as a normal signal&lt;/h3&gt;\n    @if(data.error()) {\n      Error when fetching data.\n    } @else if (data.isLoading()) {\n      Loading...\n    } @else {\n      @for(item of data.value();track $index) {\n        &lt;div&gt;#{{item.id}} - {{item.title}}&lt;/div&gt;&lt;button (click)=&quot;changeTitle($index)&quot;&gt; Change Title&lt;/button&gt;\n      }\n    }\n    &lt;h3&gt;Original data is reused without being influenced by the derived state (linkedSignal)&lt;/h3&gt;\n    &lt;select&gt;\n      @for(item of data.value();track $index) {\n        &lt;option&gt;#{{item.id}} - {{item.title}}&lt;/option&gt;\n      }\n    &lt;/select&gt;\n  `,\n})\nexport class App {\n    data = httpResource&lt;any&gt;(() =&gt; `https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos`, {\n      parse: (response: any) =&gt; {\n        // add a default element during initialization,\n        response.unshift({ id: '0', title: '&lt;&lt;Enter a value&gt;&gt;' });\n        return response.slice(0, 16);\n      },\n    });\n\n    changeTitle(index: any) {\n      this.data.update((dataList) =&gt; {\n        dataList[index].title = 'Changed ' + dataList[index].title;\n        return [...dataList];\n      });\n    }\n}\n\nbootstrapApplication(App, {\n  providers: [provideHttpClient()],\n});\n</code></pre>\n<h3><a href=\"https://stackblitz.com/edit/angular-vseqh1pk?file=src%2Fmain.ts\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Stackblitz Demo</a></h3>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"ggorlen","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/6243352/ggorlen"},"score":0,"last_activity_date":1768605896,"creation_date":1768605896,"answer_id":79869839,"question_id":67096646,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/67096646/playwrights-page-waitforresponse-differentiating-by-request-and-response-bodies/79869839#79869839","body":"<p>This would be easier to answer if the distinguishing info from the request or response bodies was provided. Nonetheless, I'll provide a minimal general example showing how to differentiate responses by both request and response bodies, since <code>waitForResponse</code>'s predicate can be async, as of <a href=\"https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/pull/6201\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PR 6201</a>.</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-js prettyprint-override\"><code>import {expect, test} from &quot;@playwright/test&quot;; // ^1.57.0\n\nconst html = `&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;&lt;html&gt;&lt;body&gt;&lt;script&gt;\nconst runSamplePost = id =&gt; {\n  return fetch(&quot;https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts&quot;, {\n    method: &quot;POST&quot;,\n    headers: {&quot;Content-Type&quot;: &quot;application/json&quot;},\n    body: JSON.stringify({id}),\n  });\n};\nrunSamplePost(1);\nrunSamplePost(2);\nrunSamplePost(3);\n&lt;/script&gt;&lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;`;\n\ntest(&quot;waitForResponse body parsing&quot;, async ({page}) =&gt; {\n  const responsePromise = page.waitForResponse(async resp =&gt; {\n    if (\n      !resp.url().includes(&quot;/posts&quot;) ||\n      resp.request().method() !== &quot;POST&quot;\n    ) {\n      return false;\n    }\n\n    const reqBody = JSON.parse(resp.request().postData() || &quot;{}&quot;);\n    if (reqBody.id !== 2) {\n      return false;\n    }\n\n    const resBody = await resp.json();\n    return resBody.id === 101;\n  });\n  await page.setContent(html);\n  const response = await responsePromise;\n  const data = await response.json();\n  expect(data.id).toBe(101);\n});\n</code></pre>\n<p>See also: <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/78124951/playwright-javascript-how-to-wait-for-a-network-request-with-a-particular-reque/78125171#78125171\">Playwright JavaScript: How to wait for a network request with a particular request payload?</a>.</p>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"mmustra","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/22676176/mmustra"},"score":1,"last_activity_date":1767824691,"last_edit_date":1767824691,"creation_date":1767823746,"answer_id":79862799,"question_id":79524149,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79524149/how-to-cancel-previous-api-calls-when-updating-query-params-in-angular-with-tans/79862799#79862799","body":"<p>Per your STR, I assume that query params changes in URL are observed via route observable that updates some signal connected to TanStack query flow.</p>\n<p>As you didn't provide minimum code example I've forked TanStack's public one, to demonstrate problem and solutions. It may differ in some ways, but the core concept should be the same.</p>\n<p><strong>Canceling problem</strong> (<a href=\"https://stackblitz.com/edit/tanstack-query-nt8par8d?file=src%2Fapp%2Fcomponents%2Fpost.component.ts\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">stackblitz</a>)</p>\n<p>Every time <code>queryKey</code> change, we send a new request without canceling the previous one.</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-ts prettyprint-override\"><code>  // assume that `this.postId()` rapidly changes\n  readonly postQuery = injectQuery(() =&gt; ({\n    enabled: this.postId() &gt; 0,\n    queryKey: ['post', this.postId()],\n    queryFn: () =&gt; {\n      return lastValueFrom(this.#postsService.postById$(this.postId()));\n    },\n  }));\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Direct abort signal solution</strong> (<a href=\"https://stackblitz.com/edit/tanstack-query-ftk1qi8w?file=src%2Fapp%2Fcomponents%2Fpost.component.ts&amp;preset=node\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">stackblitz</a>)</p>\n<p>To solve the problem we'll utilize the <code>context.signal</code> abort signal. Per <a href=\"https://tanstack.com/query/latest/docs/framework/angular/guides/query-cancellation\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">docs</a> it states:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>When a query becomes out-of-date or inactive, this signal will become aborted.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Every time <code>queryKey</code> changes, the abort signal will be triggered, and we can use it to cancel previous request.</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-ts prettyprint-override\"><code>  // canceling with `context.signal` directly in component\n  readonly postQuery = injectQuery(() =&gt; ({\n    enabled: this.postId() &gt; 0,\n    queryKey: ['post', this.postId()],\n    queryFn: (context) =&gt; {\n      const abort$ = fromEvent(context.signal, 'abort');\n\n      return lastValueFrom(\n        this.#postsService.postById$(this.postId()).pipe(takeUntil(abort$))\n      );\n    },\n  }));\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Abstracted abort signal solution</strong> (<a href=\"https://stackblitz.com/edit/tanstack-query-mywy3vzk?file=src%2Fapp%2Fservices%2Fposts-service.ts&amp;preset=node\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">stackblitz</a>)</p>\n<p>I'm not a big fan of mixing API service flow with components. So, if you want to commit to TanStack and leave space for Angular's <a href=\"https://angular.dev/guide/signals/resource\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">resources</a>/<a href=\"https://angular.dev/ecosystem/rxjs-interop#using-rxresource-for-async-data\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">rxResources</a> I recommend abstracting <code>injectQuery</code> to service and exposing only signals similar to what you would do with <code>httpClient</code> and observables. This will allow you to reuse entire flows via service in multiple components and scale/maintain logic easily.</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-ts prettyprint-override\"><code>  // canceling with `context.signal` from service method\n  postById(\n    postId: number | Signal&lt;number&gt;,\n    destroyTriggers?: DestroyTriggers\n  ): CreateQueryResult&lt;Post, Error&gt; {\n    if (!isSignal(postId)) {\n      postId = signal(postId);\n    }\n\n    const createAborters = abortFactory(destroyTriggers);\n\n    return injectQuery(() =&gt; ({\n      enabled: postId() &gt; 0,\n      queryKey: ['post', postId()],\n      queryFn: (context) =&gt; {\n        const aborters$ = createAborters(context.signal);\n\n        return lastValueFrom(\n          this.#http\n            .get&lt;Post&gt;(`https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/${postId()}`)\n            .pipe(takeUntil(aborters$))\n        );\n      },\n    }));\n  }\n</code></pre>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"Florin Dobre","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/1979861/florin-dobre"},"score":0,"last_activity_date":1765384289,"creation_date":1765384289,"answer_id":79843201,"question_id":79841217,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79841217/combine-onmomentumscrollbegin-and-abortcontroller-in-react-native-to-avoid-dupli/79843201#79843201","body":"<p>Full Flatlist code using basic functionality offered by <em>phuocantd</em>'s answer:</p>\n<pre><code>import React, { useCallback, useEffect, useRef, useState } from 'react';\nimport {\n  ActivityIndicator,\n  FlatList,\n  ListRenderItemInfo,\n  StyleSheet,\n  Text,\n  View,\n} from 'react-native';\n\nconst API_URL = 'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts';\nconst PAGE_SIZE = 20;\n\ntype Item = {\n  id: number;\n  title: string;\n  body: string;\n};\n\nasync function fetchPage(page: number, signal?: AbortSignal): Promise&lt;Item[]&gt; {\n  console.log('Fetching page:', `${API_URL}?_limit=${PAGE_SIZE}&amp;_page=${page}`, { signal });\n  const res = await fetch(`${API_URL}?_limit=${PAGE_SIZE}&amp;_page=${page}`);\n  if (!res.ok) throw new Error('Failed to fetch data');\n  return res.json();\n}\n\nconst ListItem = React.memo(({ item }: { item: Item }) =&gt; (\n  &lt;View style={styles.item}&gt;\n    &lt;Text style={styles.title}&gt;{item.title}&lt;/Text&gt;\n    &lt;Text style={styles.desc}&gt;{item.body}&lt;/Text&gt;\n  &lt;/View&gt;\n));\n\nconst InfiniteList: React.FC = () =&gt; {\n  const [data, setData] = useState&lt;Item[]&gt;([]);\n  const [page, setPage] = useState(1);\n  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);\n  const [hasMore, setHasMore] = useState(true);\n\n  const abortRef = useRef&lt;AbortController | null&gt;(null);\n  const onEndReachedCalledDuringMomentum = useRef(false);\n\n  // Initial load only once\n  useEffect(() =&gt; {\n    loadMore();\n    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\n  }, []);\n\n  const loadMore = useCallback(async () =&gt; {\n    if (loading || !hasMore) return;\n\n    setLoading(true);\n    abortRef.current?.abort();\n    const controller = new AbortController();\n    abortRef.current = controller;\n\n    try {\n      const items = await fetchPage(page, controller.signal);\n      setData((prev) =&gt; [...prev, ...items]);\n      setHasMore(items.length === PAGE_SIZE);\n      setPage((prev) =&gt; prev + 1);\n    } catch (err: any) {\n      if (err.name !== 'AbortError') console.warn('Fetch error:', err);\n    } finally {\n      setLoading(false);\n    }\n  }, [loading, hasMore, page]);\n\n  const handleEndReached = useCallback(() =&gt; {\n    if (!onEndReachedCalledDuringMomentum.current &amp;&amp; !loading &amp;&amp; hasMore) {\n      loadMore();\n      onEndReachedCalledDuringMomentum.current = true;\n    }\n  }, [loading, hasMore, loadMore]);\n\n  const keyExtractor = useCallback((item: Item) =&gt; item.id.toString(), []);\n  const renderItem = useCallback(\n    ({ item }: ListRenderItemInfo&lt;Item&gt;) =&gt; &lt;ListItem item={item} /&gt;,\n    []\n  );\n\n  const ListFooter = useCallback(\n    () =&gt;\n      loading ? (\n        &lt;View style={styles.footer}&gt;\n          &lt;ActivityIndicator color=&quot;#666&quot; size=&quot;small&quot; /&gt;\n        &lt;/View&gt;\n      ) : null,\n    [loading]\n  );\n\n  return (\n    &lt;FlatList\n      data={data}\n      keyExtractor={keyExtractor}\n      renderItem={renderItem}\n      onEndReached={handleEndReached}\n      onEndReachedThreshold={0.7}\n      onMomentumScrollBegin={() =&gt; {\n        onEndReachedCalledDuringMomentum.current = false;\n      }}\n      ListFooterComponent={ListFooter}\n      contentContainerStyle={styles.container}\n      showsVerticalScrollIndicator={false}\n      removeClippedSubviews\n      initialNumToRender={10}\n      maxToRenderPerBatch={10}\n      windowSize={10}\n    /&gt;\n  );\n};\n\nexport default InfiniteList;\n\nconst styles = StyleSheet.create({\n  container: {\n    paddingBottom: 20,\n  },\n  item: {\n    backgroundColor: '#fff',\n    marginHorizontal: 16,\n    marginVertical: 8,\n    borderRadius: 8,\n    elevation: 2,\n    padding: 12,\n  },\n  title: {\n    fontSize: 16,\n    fontWeight: '600',\n    color: '#111',\n    marginBottom: 6,\n  },\n  desc: {\n    fontSize: 14,\n    color: '#444',\n  },\n  footer: {\n    paddingVertical: 16,\n  },\n});\n</code></pre>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"Bojan Elez","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/9937374/bojan-elez"},"score":1,"last_activity_date":1765205250,"creation_date":1765205250,"answer_id":79841082,"question_id":79837738,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79837738/next-js-source-maps-error-invalid-source-map-only-conformant-source-maps-can-b/79841082#79841082","body":"<p>I had the same issue, I fixed it with await just the params</p>\n<pre><code>import { IAlbums } from &quot;@/app/types&quot;;\n\nexport default async function AlbumPage({ params }: { params: Promise&lt;{id: string}&gt; }) {\n  const id = (await params).id\n  const res = await fetch(`https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/albums/${id}`);\n  const album: IAlbums = await res.json();\n\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;p-4&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;h1 className=&quot;text-xl font-bold mb-4&quot;&gt;Album {album.id}&lt;/h1&gt;\n      &lt;p className=&quot;text-lg&quot;&gt;{album.title}&lt;/p&gt; \n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n</code></pre>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"malhal","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/259521/malhal"},"score":3,"last_activity_date":1764222026,"last_edit_date":1764222026,"creation_date":1761046201,"answer_id":79795760,"question_id":79795184,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79795184/in-swift-how-to-resolve-warning-about-main-actor-isolated-conformance-cannot-be/79795760#79795760","body":"<p>Seems you are getting in a real mess with weak self, detached etc. Async/await requires thinking a bit differently about code so I think you need to start over from scratch with the following points in mind:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p>When async is running it's essentially creating a lifetime (like an object) so you don't actually need a class anymore. So first you need to move your func outside of class. Class is for mutable state thus can't have any async instance funcs or it will corrupt the properties. This is one of the reasons Apple always stay start with struct. The way to have mutable state that is not corrupt by instance async funcs is actor but that is not the right tool for your problem, e.g. it's for implementing the model store for your whole app that saves, loads, syncs etc.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>What matters is where you define the async func, not where it is called from. Thus your async code that is inside view code, i.e. MainActor has zero chance of ever running on the background thread. Detached is not a valid solution to that because it is designed for when no UI update is required on completion, i.e. it would be valid for logging.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Async funcs usually always return a result.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Putting this in practice looks something like:</p>\n<p>e.g.</p>\n<pre><code>// enum struct or anything but not class\n// Or you could even put it as a static func in Post, e.g.\n\nextension Post {\n\n    // since Post is not isolated to an actor, this func will be too.\n    static func fetchPost() async throws -&gt; Post {\n        let url = URL(string: &quot;https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1&quot;)!\n        let (data, _) = try await URLSession.shared.data(from: url)\n        return try JSONDecoder().decode(Post.self, from: data)\n    }\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Then you can call it from <code>.task</code> as follows:</p>\n<pre><code>\n@State var post: Post?\n\n.task { // start on appear, throws cancellation exception on disappear, runs on main actor\n    do {\n        // this await will hop from main thread to background thread\n        post = try await Post.fetchPost()\n    } catch {\n        print(&quot;Failed to load: \\(error.localizedDescription)&quot;)\n      }\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can improve on this by putting <code>fetchPost</code> in an <code>EnvironmentValue</code> so then it can be replaced with a mock func for previews.</p>\n<p>To guarantee <code>Post</code> is not main actor you might have to turn off a new Xcode 26 setting which is just complicating the situation further.</p>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"Greenman","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/9615776/greenman"},"score":-1,"last_activity_date":1763733205,"last_edit_date":1763733205,"creation_date":1763730565,"answer_id":79826527,"question_id":45018338,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/45018338/javascript-fetch-api-how-to-save-output-to-variable-as-an-object-not-the-prom/79826527#79826527","body":"<p>You can kinda do it a hacky sort of way with storage...</p>\n<pre><code>(async function foo() { \n  const res = await fetch('https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1');\n  let obj = await res.json();\n  let str = JSON.stringify(obj);\n  localStorage.setItem(&quot;mydata&quot;, str);\n  return obj;\n})();\n\nlet myVar =  localStorage.getItem(&quot;mydata&quot;);\nlet v = JSON.parse(myVar);\nconsole.log(&quot;Global: &quot; + v.title);\n</code></pre>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"Naren Murali","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/5924562/naren-murali"},"score":1,"last_activity_date":1760810827,"last_edit_date":1760810827,"creation_date":1760803685,"answer_id":79793845,"question_id":79793841,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79793841/how-to-prevent-rxjs-to-stop-pipeline-execution-on-the-first-exception/79793845#79793845","body":"<p>We can move the <code>catchError</code> inside the ajax Observable using pipe. Then use <code>concatAll</code> alone for the outer pipe.</p>\n<p>The <code>catchError</code> handles the failure and ensures the API stream continues.</p>\n<pre><code>import './style.css';\n\nimport { rx, map, from, of, catchError, concatAll } from 'rxjs';\nimport { ajax } from 'rxjs/ajax';\nlet urls = from(\n  [\n    'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos/1', // good URL\n    'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todosqqwrqewr/2', // error 404\n    'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos/3', // good URL, ajax request won't execute\n  ].map((url) =&gt;\n    ajax({\n      method: 'GET',\n      url: url,\n      responseType: 'text',\n    }).pipe(\n      catchError((err: any) =&gt; {\n        console.log('error: ', err);\n        return of({});\n      })\n    )\n  )\n)\n  .pipe(concatAll())\n  .subscribe((response: any) =&gt; {\n    console.log('status: ', response);\n  });\n</code></pre>\n<h3><a href=\"https://stackblitz.com/edit/rxjs-9gnmbzzh?devtoolsheight=60&amp;file=index.ts\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Stackblitz Demo</a></h3>\n<hr />\n<h3>Using Map:</h3>\n<p>We can also achieve using the below alternative code, same concept. We move the <code>catchError</code> inside the <code>map</code> so that the inner stream is error handled, not affecting the outer main stream.</p>\n<pre><code>import './style.css';\n\nimport { rx, map, from, of, catchError, concatAll } from 'rxjs';\nimport { ajax } from 'rxjs/ajax';\nlet urls = from([\n  'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos/1', // good URL\n  'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todosqqwrqewr/2', // error 404\n  'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos/3', // good URL, ajax request won't execute\n])\n  .pipe(\n    map((url: any) =&gt;\n      ajax({\n        method: 'GET',\n        url: url,\n        responseType: 'text',\n      }).pipe(\n        catchError((err: any) =&gt; {\n          console.log('error: ', err);\n          return of({});\n        })\n      )\n    ),\n    concatAll()\n  )\n  .subscribe((response: any) =&gt; {\n    console.log('status: ', response);\n  });\n</code></pre>\n<h3><a href=\"https://stackblitz.com/edit/rxjs-8hvijmoj?devtoolsheight=60&amp;file=index.ts\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Stackblitz Demo</a></h3>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"andrewJames","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/12567365/andrewjames"},"score":0,"last_activity_date":1759332417,"last_edit_date":1759332417,"creation_date":1759330579,"answer_id":79780207,"question_id":79779364,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79779364/datatables-how-to-update-select-filters-based-on-other-select-filter-but-json-as/79780207#79780207","body":"<p>Here is a minimal runnable example, using data provided from an AJAX source (a test URL service, which provides JSON data):</p>\n<p><div class=\"snippet\" data-lang=\"js\" data-hide=\"false\" data-console=\"true\" data-babel=\"false\" data-babel-preset-react=\"false\" data-babel-preset-ts=\"false\">\n<div class=\"snippet-code\">\n<pre class=\"snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override\"><code>&lt;!doctype html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=\"en\"&gt;\n\n&lt;head&gt;\n  &lt;meta charset=\"UTF-8\"&gt;\n  &lt;title&gt;Demo&lt;/title&gt;\n\n  &lt;script src=\"https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.5.1.min.js\"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n  &lt;script src=\"https://cdn.datatables.net/1.10.21/js/jquery.dataTables.min.js\"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n  &lt;link rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" href=\"https://cdn.datatables.net/1.10.21/css/jquery.dataTables.min.css\"&gt;\n  &lt;link rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" href=\"https://datatables.net/media/css/site-examples.css\"&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n\n&lt;body&gt;\n\n  &lt;div style=\"margin: 20px;\"&gt;\n\n    &lt;table id=\"example\" class=\"display\" style=\"width:100%\"&gt;&lt;/table&gt;\n\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n\n  &lt;script&gt;\n    $(document).ready(function() {\n\n\n      var table = $('#example').DataTable({\n        orderCellsTop: true,\n        ajax: {\n          method: \"GET\",\n          url: \"https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts\",\n          dataSrc: \"\"\n        },\n        \"columns\": [{\n            \"title\": \"User ID\",\n            \"data\": \"userId\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"title\": \"ID\",\n            \"data\": \"id\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"title\": \"Title\",\n            \"data\": \"title\"\n          }\n        ],\n\n        initComplete: function() {\n          // manually create a 2nd table header row (where the select lists will be placed):\n          $('#example thead tr').after('&lt;tr&gt;&lt;th&gt;&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;&lt;/th&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;');\n\n          this.api()\n            .columns()\n            .every(function() {\n              let column = this;\n\n              // Create select element\n              let select = document.createElement('select');\n              select.add(new Option(''));\n\n              // the 2nd header row, where the selects are created:\n              var header2 = $('#example thead tr:eq(1) th')[column.index()];\n              header2.replaceChildren(select);\n\n              // Apply listener for user change in value\n              select.addEventListener('change', function() {\n                column\n                  .search(select.value, {\n                    exact: true\n                  })\n                  .draw();\n              });\n\n              // Add list of options\n              column\n                .data()\n                .unique()\n                .sort()\n                .each(function(d, j) {\n                  select.add(new Option(d));\n                });\n            });\n        }\n\n      });\n\n    });\n  &lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;/body&gt;\n\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>\n</div>\n</div>\n</p>\n<p>It is based on <a href=\"https://datatables.net/examples/api/multi_filter_select.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">this official example</a>, but adapted for AJAX-sourced data, as shown in various examples, such as <a href=\"https://datatables.net/examples/ajax/objects.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">this one</a>.</p>\n<p>This may give you some ideas for your own solution. The main point is: it uses DataTables' built-in support for Ajax, which simplifies the overall code.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Warning: In my MRE, I use <code>dataSrc: &quot;&quot;</code> because that matches the structure of the JSON provided by the URL I am using. But your JSON may have a different overall structure (at the outermost level of the JSON) - and therefore you may need to <a href=\"https://datatables.net/reference/option/ajax.dataSrc\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">change this option</a> to match your JSON.</p>\n<p>See also <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/q/64944852/12567365\">When using Ajax with jQuery DataTables, how do I determine what to do with the data returned?</a></p>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"ggorlen","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/6243352/ggorlen"},"score":1,"last_activity_date":1758295635,"last_edit_date":1758295635,"creation_date":1714006066,"answer_id":78381785,"question_id":48511357,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/48511357/how-to-scrape-json-from-puppeteer/78381785#78381785","body":"<p>Puppeteer is a browser automation tool, so if you're just making a request to get a raw JSON document, there's nothing to automate. Prefer using Node's native <code>fetch</code>, possibly with a browser user agent or cookies/headers if you're running into a server block:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-js prettyprint-override\"><code>const url = &quot;https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users&quot;;\n\nfetch(url)\n  .then(res =&gt; {\n    if (!res.ok) {\n      throw Error(res.statusText);\n    }\n\n    return res.json();\n  })\n  .then(data =&gt; {\n    console.log(data);\n  })\n  .catch(err =&gt; console.error(err));\n</code></pre>\n<p>This is faster and simpler than Puppeteer and has no dependency or install step.</p>\n<p>If you really need Puppeteer for some reason, possibly to bypass a block or include live credentials from a site, you can do it as follows:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-js prettyprint-override\"><code>const puppeteer = require(&quot;puppeteer&quot;); // ^22.6.0\n\nconst url = &quot;https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users&quot;;\n\nlet browser;\n(async () =&gt; {\n  browser = await puppeteer.launch();\n  const [page] = await browser.pages();\n  const response = await page.goto(url, {waitUntil: &quot;domcontentloaded&quot;});\n  const data = await response.json();\n  console.log(data);\n})()\n  .catch(err =&gt; console.error(err))\n  .finally(() =&gt; browser?.close());\n</code></pre>\n<p>This is better practice than existing answers: it closes the browser if an error is thrown, uses the response returned by <code>goto</code> to avoid having to use <code>page.on</code> or <code>evaluate</code>, and uses <code>&quot;domcontentloaded&quot;</code> which is the fastest navigation wait condition (<code>&quot;networkidle0&quot;</code> will unnecessarily wait for 500ms after fetching the document).</p>\n<p>Speed comparison. Fetch:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-none prettyprint-override\"><code>real 0m0.474s\nuser 0m0.131s\nsys  0m0.049s\n</code></pre>\n<p>Puppeteer:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-none prettyprint-override\"><code>real 0m0.662s\nuser 0m0.544s\nsys  0m0.134s\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another trick to keep in mind about fetch: it works in the browser, which can be useful for cases when you need to hit a protected API with same origin credentials from the page, imitating a call from the client. In this case, you can run <code>fetch</code> from an <code>evaluate</code> block.</p>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"Hossein Shafiei","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/6751998/hossein-shafiei"},"score":0,"last_activity_date":1755959146,"creation_date":1755959146,"answer_id":79744329,"question_id":38673673,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/38673673/access-http-response-as-string-in-go/79744329#79744329","body":"<p>let me explain it with an example</p>\n<pre><code>func GetAllTasks() {\n    tasks := make(chan string)\n    for i := 1; i &lt;= 100; i++ {\n        go GetTask(i, tasks)\n    }\n\n    for i := 1; i &lt;= 100; i++ {\n        print(&lt;-tasks)\n    }\n}\n\nfunc GetTask(id int, tasks chan&lt;- string) {\n    res, err := http.Get(&quot;https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos/&quot; + strconv.Itoa(id))\n    if err != nil {\n        panic(err)\n    }\n    body, err := io.ReadAll(res.Body)\n    if err != nil {\n        panic(err)\n    }\n    tasks &lt;- string(body)\n}\n\nfunc main() {\n    GetAllTasks()\n}\n</code></pre>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"Topaco","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/9014097/topaco"},"score":1,"last_activity_date":1752746282,"creation_date":1752746282,"answer_id":79704626,"question_id":64786678,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/64786678/javascript-equivalent-to-java-sha1prng/79704626#79704626","body":"<p>To my knowledge, there is no document that specifies the SHA1PRNG algorithm. The closest thing to a specification is Sun's implementation. Subsequent providers have made their own changes. Therefore, the SHA1PRNG algorithm is implemented non-uniformly across platforms (and sometimes even across versions) as is the case e.g. for Android and Sun.</p>\n<p>Since the question is about Sun's SHA1PRNG implementation, the most reliable solution is to port the Sun logic. A possible lightweight port based on <a href=\"https://github.com/openjdk/jdk/blob/jdk-24%2B36/src/java.base/share/classes/sun/security/provider/SecureRandom.java\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">JDK 24</a> is:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-js prettyprint-override\"><code>class Sha1PrngLW {\n    static DIGEST_SIZE = 20;\n    constructor(seed) {\n        this.state = Sha1PrngLW.sha1(seed);\n        this.remainder = new Uint8Array(Sha1PrngLW.DIGEST_SIZE);\n        this.remCount = 0;\n    }\n\n    getNextBytes(number) {\n        const result = new Uint8Array(number);\n        let index = 0;\n        let todo;\n        let output = this.remainder;\n\n        let r = this.remCount;\n        if (r &gt; 0) {\n            todo = Math.min(result.length - index, Sha1PrngLW.DIGEST_SIZE - r);\n            for (let i = 0; i &lt; todo; i++) {\n                result[i] = output[r];\n                output[r++] = 0;\n            }\n            this.remCount += todo;\n            index += todo;\n        }\n\n        while (index &lt; result.length) {\n            output = Sha1PrngLW.sha1(this.state);\n            this.updateState(this.state, output);\n\n            todo = Math.min((result.length - index), Sha1PrngLW.DIGEST_SIZE);\n            for (let i = 0; i &lt; todo; i++) {\n                result[index++] = output[i];\n                output[i] = 0;\n            }\n            this.remCount += todo;\n        }\n\n        this.remainder = output;\n        this.remCount %= Sha1PrngLW.DIGEST_SIZE;\n\n        return result;\n    }\n    \n    updateState(state, output) {\n        let last = 1;\n        let v;\n        let t;\n        let zf = false;\n    \n        for (let i = 0; i &lt; state.length; i++) {\n            v = Sha1PrngLW.toSByte(state[i]) + Sha1PrngLW.toSByte(output[i]) + last;\n            t = v &amp; 0xFF; \n            zf = zf || (state[i] !== t);\n            state[i] = t;\n            last = v &gt;&gt; 8;\n        }\n    \n        if (!zf) {\n            state[0]++;\n        }\n    }\n\n    static toSByte(byte) {\n        return byte &gt; 127 ? byte - 256 : byte;\n    }\n\n    static sha1(data) {\n        return new Uint8Array(sha1.create().update(data).arrayBuffer()); // apply your preferred SHA-1 implementation\n    }\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The code above uses the <a href=\"https://github.com/emn178/js-sha1\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">js-sha1</a> implementation for SHA-1, which can of course be replaced by one of your preference (e.g. CryptoJS).</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Use case - Creation of a 32 bytes key for AES-256:</p>\n<p><div class=\"snippet\" data-lang=\"js\" data-hide=\"true\" data-console=\"true\" data-babel=\"false\" data-babel-preset-react=\"false\" data-babel-preset-ts=\"false\">\n<div class=\"snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden\">\n<pre class=\"snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override\"><code>class Sha1PrngLW {\n    static DIGEST_SIZE = 20;\n    constructor(seed) {\n        this.state = Sha1PrngLW.sha1(seed);\n        this.remainder = new Uint8Array(Sha1PrngLW.DIGEST_SIZE);\n        this.remCount = 0;\n    }\n\n    getNextBytes(number) {\n        const result = new Uint8Array(number);\n        let index = 0;\n        let todo;\n        let output = this.remainder;\n\n        let r = this.remCount;\n        if (r &gt; 0) {\n            todo = Math.min(result.length - index, Sha1PrngLW.DIGEST_SIZE - r);\n            for (let i = 0; i &lt; todo; i++) {\n                result[i] = output[r];\n                output[r++] = 0;\n            }\n            this.remCount += todo;\n            index += todo;\n        }\n\n        while (index &lt; result.length) {\n            output = Sha1PrngLW.sha1(this.state);\n            this.updateState(this.state, output);\n\n            todo = Math.min((result.length - index), Sha1PrngLW.DIGEST_SIZE);\n            for (let i = 0; i &lt; todo; i++) {\n                result[index++] = output[i];\n                output[i] = 0;\n            }\n            this.remCount += todo;\n        }\n\n        this.remainder = output;\n        this.remCount %= Sha1PrngLW.DIGEST_SIZE;\n\n        return result;\n    }\n    \n    updateState(state, output) {\n        let last = 1;\n        let v;\n        let t;\n        let zf = false;\n    \n        for (let i = 0; i &lt; state.length; i++) {\n            v = Sha1PrngLW.toSByte(state[i]) + Sha1PrngLW.toSByte(output[i]) + last;\n            t = v &amp; 0xFF; \n            zf = zf || (state[i] !== t);\n            state[i] = t;\n            last = v &gt;&gt; 8;\n        }\n    \n        if (!zf) {\n            state[0]++;\n        }\n    }\n\n    static toSByte(byte) {\n        return byte &gt; 127 ? byte - 256 : byte;\n    }\n\n    static sha1(data) {\n        return new Uint8Array(sha1.create().update(data).arrayBuffer()); // apply your preferred SHA-1 implementation\n    }\n}\n\nvar seed = hex2ab(\"0099447669d26c259ab0bb399cdf6d40c80afca162bfb7aae8354c9ba6fb594d2d323657c958763a19407f0be2e6cc23\");\nvar prng = new Sha1PrngLW(seed);\nvar nextBytes = prng.getNextBytes(32);\nconsole.log(ab2hex(nextBytes)); // e92c65d5e0ab7400578756d62920f7f5206a70f5760c40819ed7c27682347c87\n\nvar prng = new Sha1PrngLW(seed);\nvar nextBytes1 = prng.getNextBytes(10);\nvar nextBytes2 = prng.getNextBytes(22);\nconsole.log(ab2hex(nextBytes1) + ab2hex(nextBytes2)); // e92c65d5e0ab7400578756d62920f7f5206a70f5760c40819ed7c27682347c87\n\n// Helper\nfunction ab2hex(ab) { \n    return Array.prototype.map.call(new Uint8Array(ab), x =&gt; ('00' + x.toString(16)).slice(-2)).join('');\n}\n\nfunction hex2ab(hex){\n    return new Uint8Array(hex.match(/[\\da-f]{2}/gi).map(function (h) {return parseInt(h, 16)}));\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override\"><code>&lt;script src=\"https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/js-sha1/0.7.0/sha1.min.js\"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>\n</div>\n</div>\n</p>\n<p>The results obtained correspond to those of the Java code (tested with Java 8). Also note that the first 20 bytes correspond to the double SHA-1 hash of the seed.</p>\n<hr />\n<p><em>Why and under what conditions does this logic correspond to double SHA-1 hashing?</em></p>\n<p>Looking at the code, you can see that first an initial SHA-1 hashing of the seed is performed in the constructor. The hash is stored in <code>state</code>.<br />\nIn the <code>while</code>-loop, <code>state</code> is hashed a second time with SHA-1. The result is stored in <code>output</code>, the length of which corresponds to the SHA-1 output length of 20 bytes.<br />\nThen <code>state</code> is updated for the next iteration step in <code>updateState()</code>. However, the next iteration only takes place if the specified output length <code>result.Length</code> (= <code>number</code>) is greater than the SHA-1 output length, i.e. 20 bytes. As long as this is not the case, there is only one iteration step and the logic simplifies to a double hashing with SHA-1.</p>\n<p>Since your use case applies AES-128, i.e. a key length of 16 bytes, which is smaller than the SHA-1 output length, the same key can therefore also be generated by double hashing with SHA-1 and then shortening the byte sequence to 16 bytes.<br />\nOf course, this does not work for AES-256, as its key length of 32 bytes is greater than the SHA-1 output length of 20 bytes.</p>\n<p>For the sake of completeness: The Sun implementation allows the byte sequence to be determined in <em>multiple</em> <code>getNextBytes()</code> calls, i.e. for <code>n = n1 + n2</code>, <code>getNextBytes(n)</code> corresponds to the concatenation of <code>getNextBytes(n1)</code> and <code>getNextBytes(n2)</code>. This is the reason for the additional complexity of the implementation (which requires e.g. <code>remainder</code>, <code>remCount</code> and the <code>if (r &gt; 0)</code>-branch).</p>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"Victor Levitskiy","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/16246411/victor-levitskiy"},"score":0,"last_activity_date":1724937037,"last_edit_date":1724937037,"creation_date":1724743195,"answer_id":78917586,"question_id":78916317,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/78916317/how-to-prevent-swagger-from-crashing-when-receiving-huge-responses/78917586#78917586","body":"<p>It seems springdoc has only syntaxHighlight config for speeding up ui, as it's not helping i can suggest:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Write custom OpenApiCustomizer bean, override customise(OpenAPI openApi) method, like this</li>\n</ol>\n<pre class=\"lang-java prettyprint-override\"><code>@Override\npublic void customise(OpenAPI openApi) {\n    openApi.getComponents().getSchemas().forEach((s, schema) -&gt; {\n                // check example length\n                if (schema.getExample().toString().length() &gt; 1000L) {\n                    schema.setExample(&quot;download example here: http://example.com&quot;);\n                }\n            });\n</code></pre>\n<ol start=\"2\">\n<li><p>If the problem is only in UI and not in swagger json/yaml file, you can customize ui css and js, example is here\n<a href=\"https://github.com/springdoc/springdoc-openapi/issues/737\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">https://github.com/springdoc/springdoc-openapi/issues/737</a>\nIt's somewhat hacky, but can help</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Write an issue on <a href=\"https://github.com/springdoc/springdoc-openapi\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">https://github.com/springdoc/springdoc-openapi</a>, suggest solution, or write one yourself</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"Karl","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/10044560/karl"},"score":1,"last_activity_date":1724458864,"creation_date":1724458864,"answer_id":78907866,"question_id":78906991,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/78906991/train-an-nn-model-independently-for-different-input-versions/78907866#78907866","body":"<p>I think you are confused about some very basic programming aspects here. Lets step through some things.</p>\n<p>You create the model:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-py prettyprint-override\"><code>top_model = torch_top_model()\n</code></pre>\n<p>At this point you have one single model. Now you create this dict:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-py prettyprint-override\"><code>weights_and_biases = {i:top_model.state_dict() for i in range(1,5)}\n</code></pre>\n<p>At this point you still have one model. The entries in <code>weights_and_biases</code> all reference the same model state dict. The dict values are all pointers to the exact same object.</p>\n<p>You create the optimizer dict</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-py prettyprint-override\"><code>optimizer = {i:torch.optim.Adam(top_model.parameters(), lr=0.01) for i in range(1,5)}\n</code></pre>\n<p>At this point you still have one model. Each dict value is a different optimizer object, but they all reference the same weight objects.</p>\n<p>You create the model copy dict</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-py prettyprint-override\"><code>top_models = {i:copy.deepcopy(top_model) for i in range(1,5)}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Now you have created four new models. You have five models total - four in <code>top_models</code> plus the original model you cloned from.</p>\n<p>Now your train loop</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-py prettyprint-override\"><code>top_models[key].load_state_dict(weights_and_biases[key])\noutputs = top_models[key](inputs[key])\nloss = criterion(outputs, model_labels)\noptimizer[key].zero_grad()\nloss.backward()\n</code></pre>\n<p>If you understand the code you wrote, you can see clearly why this doesn't work. You use the model in <code>top_models[key]</code> with the optimizer <code>optimizer[key]</code>. <code>top_models[key]</code> points to one of the model copies you created. <code>optimizer[key]</code> points to the weights of the original model. They are referencing different objects. Loading the state dict via <code>top_models[key].load_state_dict(weights_and_biases[key])</code> does not change this because it copies the data values from <code>weights_and_biases[key]</code> into the tensor objects in <code>top_models[key].load_state_dict</code>.</p>\n<p>The reason you see no change in the weights is because you aren't updating them. You compute your loss with the weights in <code>top_models[key]</code>, but your optimizer references the weights in the original <code>top_model</code>. There is no connection between the two. If you actually inspect a gradient value of a parameter in the optimizer (<code>optimizer[key].param_groups[0]['params'][0].grad</code>) you will find it is <code>None</code> because no gradient is being computed. The parameters in the optimizer are unrelated to the parameters you use to calculate your loss.</p>\n<p>I think you are also confused about what you are trying to accomplish. When you say <code>I would like the model to update only weights_and_biases[1] for inputs[1], weights_and_biases[2] for inputs[2]</code>, it implies you want to train separate models on the different inputs. When you say <code>If this can be achieved without deep copying the model</code>, it implies you want a single model.</p>\n<p>You can train a single model on all versions of the input, or you can train separate instances of the model on the separate inputs.</p>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"hector-j-rivas","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/1687555/hector-j-rivas"},"score":0,"last_activity_date":1721706235,"last_edit_date":1721706235,"creation_date":1721248753,"answer_id":78761532,"question_id":78760791,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/78760791/how-to-implement-text-highlighting-in-a-console-text-editor-c/78761532#78761532","body":"<p>Writing a syntax parser is meta-djin thinking <em>and</em> the ultimate case of Hofstadter's law, <em>especially</em> for HTML :)</p>\n<p>Here's a few pointers, having wrote both token-based and regex-based parsers (and syntax <em>declaration</em> parsers) in different languages and stacks (say desktop vs. web/cloud).</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You're writing a state machine regardless of your parsing preference, and even before the user presses another key, never mind copying &amp; pasting blocks of code. That means you need <em>strategies</em> for current state (at rest) and for each event or type of event.</li>\n<li>Regex-pattern (pun intended) does get you into performance considerations as explained in <a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/base-types/best-practices-regex#interpreted-vs-source-generated-vs-compiled-regular-expressions\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Interpreted vs. source-generated vs. compiled regular expressions</a>. The learning curve is steeper, but patterns achieve greater encapsulation in syntax <em>rules</em> (another object in your model).</li>\n<li>The token-based approach is for single-character tokens (ahem, tokens). It is fast, but it multiplies states sensibly and makes it harder to express and/or interpret syntax rules.</li>\n<li>Don't append to a string, use a string builder instead.</li>\n<li>The state machine is a giant switch onion that layers spans with styles, say, if '&lt;' push a <strong>tag</strong> span to the stack at that position, if '=' push the <strong>equal</strong> span and if '&gt;' close the span, and so on. This is faster than traversing your dictionary keys.</li>\n<li>There's quite a few open-source highlighters out there; do reinvent the wheel if you aim to learn, do not if you have a timeline and a paycheck associated to this :). The ultimate C# full blown code base is <a href=\"https://github.com/icsharpcode/AvalonEdit\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Avalon Edit</a>, and I've written extensively about a simple HTML/JS/CSS OOP approach (with working samples) at <a href=\"https://cloudkitects.github.io/hectorjrivas/syntaxhighlighting.1.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">github/hectorjrivas/syntaxhighlighting</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"Topaco","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/9014097/topaco"},"score":2,"last_activity_date":1715276254,"creation_date":1715276254,"answer_id":78456106,"question_id":78440733,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/78440733/how-can-i-port-this-java-aes-gcm-nopadding-encryption-algorithm-code-to-c/78456106#78456106","body":"<p>A pseudorandom number generator (PRNG) should not be used as a key derivation function (KDF), as the implementations can vary across platforms and even versions, so that even with identical seeds it is not guaranteed that the same byte sequence will be generated. This makes a PRNG unsuitable as a KDF.</p>\n<p>The BouncyCastle implementation you are using generates a random start seed by default and merely adds the additional seed specified with <code>SetSeed()</code>. The default thus generates a random byte sequence and is therefore unsuitable for your purposes.<br />\nHowever, the implementation allows you to disable the generation of the start seed with <code>SecureRandom.GetInstance(&quot;SHA1PRNG&quot;, false)</code>. Unfortunately, the generated byte sequence does not correspond to the value of the Java implementation despite the identical seed. The reason for this is the above-mentioned non-standardized implementation of the SHA1PRNG algorithm.</p>\n<p>To avoid such compatibility issues, the Java implementation of SHA1PRNG <em>itself</em> can be ported to C#. The following C# lightweight implementation only ports the part that is needed to generate a byte sequence of arbitrary length for a given seed. In particular, the porting of the seed generation, which is not required here, was omitted. The Java source code used for the porting is <a href=\"https://github.com/openjdk/jdk/blob/jdk-22%2B36/src/java.base/share/classes/sun/security/provider/SecureRandom.java\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">JDK 22</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-cs prettyprint-override\"><code>using System.Security.Cryptography;\n...\nclass Sha1PrngLW\n{\n    private static int DIGEST_SIZE = 20;\n    private byte[] state;\n    private byte[] remainder;\n    private int remCount;\n\n    public Sha1PrngLW(byte[] seed)\n    {\n        state = SHA1.HashData(seed);\n    }\n\n    public byte[] GetNextBytes(int number)\n    {\n        byte[] result = new byte[number];\n        int index = 0;\n        int todo;\n        byte[] output = remainder;\n\n        int r = remCount;\n        if (r &gt; 0)\n        {\n            todo = Math.Min(result.Length - index, DIGEST_SIZE - r);\n            for (int i = 0; i &lt; todo; i++)\n            {\n                result[i] = output[r];\n                output[r++] = 0;\n            }\n            remCount += todo;\n            index += todo;\n        }\n\n        while (index &lt; result.Length)\n        {\n            output = SHA1.HashData(state);\n            updateState(state, output);\n\n            todo = Math.Min((result.Length - index), DIGEST_SIZE);\n            for (int i = 0; i &lt; todo; i++)\n            {\n                result[index++] = output[i];\n                output[i] = 0;\n            }\n            remCount += todo;\n        }\n\n        remainder = output;\n        remCount %= DIGEST_SIZE;\n\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    private static void updateState(byte[] state, byte[] output)\n    {\n        int last = 1;\n        int v;\n        byte t;\n        bool zf = false;\n\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; state.Length; i++)\n        {\n            v = (sbyte)state[i] + (sbyte)output[i] + last;\n            t = (byte)v;\n            zf = zf | (state[i] != t);\n            state[i] = t;\n            last = v &gt;&gt; 8;\n        }\n\n        if (!zf)\n        {\n            state[0]++;\n        }\n    }\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>A similar port can be found <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/a/70589648/9014097\">here</a>. The above port differs in some respects, as it is based on a newer Java version and is adapted to .NET 7.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Regarding the AES/GCM encryption, there is no need to use BouncyCastle on .NET 7, as this algorithm is supported with the built-in <a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.security.cryptography.aesgcm\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>AesGcm</code></a> class:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-cs prettyprint-override\"><code>Sha1PrngLW sha1PrngLW = new Sha1PrngLW(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(&quot;esp4tgsltzA2fml2&quot;));\nbyte[] key = sha1PrngLW.GetNextBytes(16);\n\nbyte[] data = Convert.FromBase64String(&quot;cOYavtcA/pvUNgU1tYiNzEOOnazFVsfce/ApmHz0gAsq&quot;);\n\nAesGcm aesGcm = new AesGcm(key);\nbyte[] decrypted = new byte[data.Length - 12 - 16];\naesGcm.Decrypt(data[0..12], data[12..^16], data[^16..], decrypted);\n\nConsole.WriteLine(Encoding.UTF8.GetString(decrypted)); // 12345\n</code></pre>\n<p>With this, the decryption is successful and returns <code>12345</code> as decrypted data.</p>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"Unmitigated","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/9513184/unmitigated"},"score":1,"last_activity_date":1715221897,"creation_date":1715221897,"answer_id":78451963,"question_id":78451221,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/78451221/bfs-implementation-takes-too-much-time-for-cses-labyrinth-problem/78451963#78451963","body":"<p>You could simply modify your code to use <code>push_back</code> (which gives the path from A to B reversed), then iterate over <code>final_path</code> in reverse.</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-cpp prettyprint-override\"><code>#include &lt;ranges&gt;\n// ...\nfor (auto i : std::views::reverse(final_path)) cout &lt;&lt; i;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Alternatively, use <code>std::deque</code> instead of <code>std::vector</code> to store the path as it has constant time <code>push_front</code>.</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-cpp prettyprint-override\"><code>#include &lt;deque&gt;\n// ...\ndeque&lt;char&gt; final_path;\n// example:\nfinal_path.push_front('R');\n</code></pre>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"trincot","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/5459839/trincot"},"score":1,"last_activity_date":1715204958,"creation_date":1715204958,"answer_id":78451295,"question_id":78451221,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/78451221/bfs-implementation-takes-too-much-time-for-cses-labyrinth-problem/78451295#78451295","body":"<p><code>final_path.insert</code> is not efficient, it has a O(𝑛) time complexity where 𝑛 is the size of <code>final_path</code>. As you need to call this repeatedly, it amounts to a time complexity of O(𝑛²) where 𝑛 is the length of the found path.</p>\n<p>For better efficiency, start your search from B and find A, so in the opposite direction. Then you can use <code>final_path.push_back</code> which has better efficiency -- amortised O(1) in your case, which means the complete path is built in O(𝑛) time complexity.</p>\n<p>When you implement this change, make sure to store the letters in the path as if you were traversing it from A to B.</p>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"Karl","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/10044560/karl"},"score":-1,"last_activity_date":1714671406,"creation_date":1714671406,"answer_id":78420856,"question_id":78392429,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/78392429/how-can-we-capture-update-done-with-optimizer-step-in-an-elegant-way/78420856#78420856","body":"<p>Probably the easiest way to do this is to dump the param groups and state dict of the optimizer after every <code>backward</code> call. This will let you capture the parameters, gradients and optimizer state.</p>\n<p>The code below shows an example with a simple MLP, but the <code>log_state</code> function should work for all models so long as the param groups/state dict don't have multiple levels of nesting.</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-py prettyprint-override\"><code>import torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\n\n# example model\nclass MLP(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, input_size, hidden_size, output_size):\n        super(MLP, self).__init__()\n        self.fc1 = nn.Linear(input_size, hidden_size)\n        self.relu = nn.ReLU()\n        self.fc2 = nn.Linear(hidden_size, output_size)\n        \n    def forward(self, x):\n        h = self.fc1(x)\n        pred = self.fc2(self.relu(h))\n        return pred\n\ndef log_state(opt):\n    output = {}\n\n    # log state dict\n    state_dict = {}\n    for key, value in opt.state_dict().items():\n        if key == 'state':\n            state_dict[key] = {}\n            for state_key, state_value in value.items():\n                state_dict[key][state_key] = {}\n                for k, v in state_value.items():\n                    if torch.is_tensor(v):\n                        state_dict[key][state_key][k] = v.cpu().clone() # move tensor to cpu\n                    else:\n                        state_dict[key][state_key][k] = v\n        else:\n            state_dict[key] = value\n\n    output['state_dict'] = state_dict\n\n    # log param groups\n    param_groups = []\n    for group in opt.param_groups:\n        param_group = {key: value for key, value in group.items() if key != 'params'}\n        param_group['params'] = []\n        param_group['param_grads'] = []\n        for param in group['params']:\n            param_group['params'].append(param.data.cpu().clone()) # move tensor to cpu\n            # log gradients\n            if param.grad is not None:\n                param_group['param_grads'].append(param.grad.data.cpu().clone()) # move tensor to cpu\n            else:\n                param_group['param_grads'].append(None)\n\n        param_groups.append(param_group)\n\n    output['param_groups'] = param_groups\n    \n    return output\n\n\n\nnet = MLP(64, 20, 10)\n\nopt = torch.optim.Adam(net.parameters(), lr=1e-3)\n\nstate_log = {}\nfor i in range(5):\n    x = torch.randn(8, 64)\n    y = torch.randn(8,10)\n    p = net(x)\n    loss = nn.functional.mse_loss(p, y)\n    opt.zero_grad()\n    loss.backward()\n    state_log[i] = log_state(opt) # log optimizer state every step\n    opt.step()\n</code></pre>\n<p>The code logs after <code>backward</code> to grab gradients. You could add additional logging after <code>step</code>, but the values updated after <code>step</code> (params, optimizer averages, etc) will be captured on the next iteration. You can also use values from one iteration to recreate the next. For example, with Adam:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-py prettyprint-override\"><code>step = 2\n\nold_state = state_log[step]\nnew_state = state_log[step+1]\n\n# adam params\nb1 = 0.9\nb2 = 0.999\neps = 1e-8\nlr = 1e-3\n\nw = old_state['param_groups'][0]['params'][0]\ng = old_state['param_groups'][0]['param_grads'][0]\n\nm_old = old_state['state_dict']['state'][0]['exp_avg']\nv_old = old_state['state_dict']['state'][0]['exp_avg_sq']\n\nm = b1 * m_old + (1-b1)*g\nv = b2 * v_old + (1-b2)*(g.pow(2))\n\nm_hat = m.div(1-b1**(step+1))\nv_hat = v.div(1-b2**(step+1))\n\nw_new = w - lr * m_hat / (torch.sqrt(v_hat) + eps)\n\ntorch.allclose(w_new, new_state['param_groups'][0]['params'][0])\n&gt; True\n</code></pre>\n"},{"owner":{"display_name":"Rub&#233;n Pozo","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/3782128/rub%c3%a9n-pozo"},"score":0,"last_activity_date":1710775218,"last_edit_date":1710775218,"creation_date":1710425106,"answer_id":78161108,"question_id":71927889,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/71927889/parse-yaml-with-dots-delimiter-in-keys/78161108#78161108","body":"<p>I have found an alternative solution with pyyaml. The strategy is convert the dict into a string with the format &quot;k1.k2.k3: value&quot; and then convert it into a dict. Probably not the most efficient, but it works.</p>\n<pre><code>import yaml\nfrom copy import deepcopy\n\nmy_yaml = &quot;&quot;&quot;\nservice.scalingPolicy.capacity:\n    min: 1\n    max: 50\n&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n\nclass YamlUtils:\n\n    def yaml_dict_to_text(self, yaml_dict, parent_key, yaml_text):\n        return_value = yaml_text.split('\\n')\n        for key, value in yaml_dict.items():\n            key_string = key\n            if parent_key != &quot;&quot;:\n                key_string = parent_key + &quot;.&quot; + key_string\n            if isinstance(value, dict):\n                return_value.append(self.yaml_dict_to_text(value, key_string, yaml_text))\n            else:\n                return_value.append(&quot;{0}: {1}&quot;.format(\n                    key_string,\n                    value\n                )\n                )\n        return '\\n'.join(return_value)\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def convert_to_dict(source_string, split_symbol='.', value=None):\n        return_value = value\n        elements = source_string.split(split_symbol)\n        for element in reversed(elements):\n            if element:\n                return_value = {element: return_value}\n        return return_value\n\n    def dict_of_dicts_merge(self, x, y):\n        z = {}\n        try:\n            overlapping_keys = x.keys() &amp; y.keys()\n            for key in overlapping_keys:\n                z[key] = self.dict_of_dicts_merge(x[key], y[key])\n            for key in x.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n                z[key] = deepcopy(x[key])\n            for key in y.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n                z[key] = deepcopy(y[key])\n        except Exception as e:\n            print(&quot;Error merging dicts:&quot;, x, y, str(e))\n        return z\n\n    def text_to_yaml_dict(self, yaml_text):\n        return_value = {}\n        yaml_list = yaml_text.split('\\n')\n        for line in yaml_list:\n            line_items = line.split(':')\n            if len(line_items) &gt;= 2:\n                line_key = line_items[0]\n                line_value = line_items[1].lstrip()\n                line_dict = self.convert_to_dict(line_key, '.', line_value)\n                return_value = self.dict_of_dicts_merge(return_value, line_dict)\n        return return_value\n\n\ndef main():\n    try:\n        yaml_dict = yaml.safe_load(my_yaml)\n        yaml_utils = YamlUtils()\n        processed_yaml_text = yaml_utils.yaml_dict_to_text(\n            yaml_dict,\n            &quot;&quot;,\n            &quot;&quot;,\n        )\n        processed_yaml_dict = yaml_utils.text_to_yaml_dict(processed_yaml_text)\n        print(&quot;Min:&quot;, processed_yaml_dict['service']['scalingPolicy']['capacity']['min'])\n        print(&quot;Max:&quot;, processed_yaml_dict['service']['scalingPolicy']['capacity']['max'])\n        data = yaml.dump(processed_yaml_dict, indent=True)\n        print(&quot;New yaml:&quot;, data)\n    except yaml.YAMLError as exc:\n        print(exc)\n\n\nif __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:\n    main()\n</code></pre>\n"}],"has_more":false,"quota_max":300,"quota_remaining":239}